


Anchorhead
an interactive Lovecraftian gothic by Michael Gentry
Release 1 / Serial number 171017 / Inform 7 build 6M62 (I6/v6.33 lib 6/12N) 
Illustrated Edition
Type ABOUT for more information about this game.

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>se

Garbage-Choked Alley
This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence.

A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder is retracted, its lowest rung hanging about ten feet up, above one of the garbage cans.

>climb garbage can
You clamber onto the wobbling garbage can, precariously balanced. The bottom rung of the fire escape is now just a few feet above your head.

>pull rung with umbrella
(first closing the umbrella)
You hook the handle of your umbrella around the bottom rung and put your whole weight on it. With a sudden, ratcheting crash that nearly knocks you off the garbage can, the ladder slides down and locks into place.

Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning.

You are getting wet.

>climb ladder
The entire fire escape rattles ominously as you shimmy up the ladder.

Fire Escape
A shaky metal platform bolted to crumbling brick, about fifteen feet up. There is a window here at waist height, big enough for you to crawl through.

A metal ladder provides access to the alley below.

You are getting wet.

>enter window
For a moment you are sure it will be locked, but to your surprise the window slides up easily.

You hesitate. Breaking and entering is not your preferred way to spend a rainy afternoon, but the real estate agent was supposed to be here and dammit, you need those keys. Surely the judge will show leniency once you explain everything. With a sigh, you slip in through the window.

File Room
Peering through the murk, you can make out the blocky outlines of filing cabinets lining the walls. A narrow stairway leads down to the office proper, and a window looks out onto a fire escape to the south.

>d

Office
Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and a glass-paneled door leads west to the street.

Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine.

Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold.

>x answering machine
A simple digital answering machine, with a button labeled PLAY, a button labeled DELETE, and a small display indicating messages received. The display indicates there is one message on the machine.

>press play
For a while there is nothing but a quiet hiss, followed by intermittent skirls of static. It sounds like one of those annoying glitches where the caller hangs up but the machine keeps recording anyway. Then, barely audible through the static, you detect what sounds like a human voice whispering a single word:

"Verlac."

The machine beeps.

A shudder ripples up your back. You remember now, "Verlac" is the name of this branch of Michael's family.

You hear a fly buzzing around somewhere in the room.

>u

File Room
Peering through the murk, you can make out the blocky outlines of filing cabinets lining the walls. A narrow stairway leads down to the office proper, and a window looks out onto a fire escape to the south.

>search cabinets
You find the file for the Verlac property easily enough — but the file is empty. Title, deed, all the papers, all of it gone. In the back of the drawer, however, is a set of keys that must have slipped out of the hanging folder. Relieved, you pocket them.

>d

Office
Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and a glass-paneled door leads west to the street.

Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine.

Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold.

>w
You turn the latch, unlocking the glass-paneled door.

You open the glass-paneled door.

The rain is still coming down, so you open your umbrella.

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>se

Garbage-Choked Alley
This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence.

A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder dangles just a few feet above the cobblestones.

>x fence
One of the boards seems to be loose down at the bottom. You could probably squeeze through.

The rain beats down ceaselessly.

>e
Dropping to your hands and knees, you wriggle underneath the loose board and scramble down a muddy slope.

Littered Beach
A narrow strip of sand tucked away between two outcroppings in the rocky shoreline, accessible only by way of a steep, muddy slope to the west. The sand is filthy and strewn with rocks, seaweed, and other bits of storm-tossed detritus.

Near the bottom of the slope, a sewage outflow pipe juts out over the beach, about three feet above the ground. A thin stream of acrid-smelling sewage trickles out over the lip of the pipe, forming a puddle in the sand.

>w
You pick your way up the slope, push the loose board aside, and slip back in through the gap.

Garbage-Choked Alley
This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence.

A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder dangles just a few feet above the cobblestones.

The clouds overhead mutter restlessly to themselves.

>nw

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>w

Narrow Street
As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

A sudden gust blows a cold spray of rain into your face.

>w

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

>nw

University Courtyard
The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library, where Michael told you he'd be until he came back to the real estate agent's office to pick you up — which, you cannot help but note with some annoyance, he has not yet done.

You recall Michael describing this place on the long drive up here. Founded in the late 1800s, the university represents this benighted town's single, if dubious, claim to cultural achievement. The school's reputation and enrollment have diminished over the years as Anchorhead drifts further and further into the abyss of provincial backwaterism, but the library, at least, is still recognized for its collection of folklore and esoteric mythology. And the Board of Deans was kind enough to offer your husband a tenured position upon hearing of his recently discovered heritage and his plans to move into the estate. Not exactly Ivy League, but they take do care of their own.

>w
You close your umbrella as you step inside.

Reading Room
Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk.

Peering through the dimness, you spot your husband at one of the reading desks, absorbed in some sort of weighty tome and clearly oblivious to the time.

>hello
Michael looks up, startled, then realizes it's you. "Hi hon," he says. He closes the book and stretches. "Have you been waiting a long time? I'm so sorry — I just got absorbed here and lost track of time." He smiles sheepishly.

"Well?" he asks. "Did you get the keys?"

>yes
"Great! Let's see 'em."

>show keys to michael
(first taking the keyring from your pocket)
"Excellent!" says Michael. "You hold on to that, I'll go put this book away."

Michael goes north.

>n

Circulation Desk
A high counter divides the reading area from the stacks. A small sign affixed to the wall next to the counter provides instructions for those seeking to check out books.

On the counter are a service bell and a dog-eared register.

Michael is here.

Michael rings the bell. A moment later, the gaunt and fishy-eyed librarian emerges silently from the shadows behind the counter.

>x book
Michael's hand is on the cover, partially obscuring its title.

Michael hands the book over to the librarian, who retreats with it into the depths of the stacks.

Michael goes south.

>s

Reading Room
Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk.

Michael is here, waiting for you.

Michael suddenly smacks his forehead. "Oh, crap, I forgot to tell you. The car broke down." He spreads his hands. "I don't know what happened to it; one second I'm parking outside, the next second it dies and I can't get it started again. I called a tow truck, but the nearest garage is over in Innswich. They took the car but won't be able to look at it until next week at the earliest."

He offers you a lame smile. "I guess we're walking home. You can hold the umbrella."

>e
The rain is still coming down, so you open your umbrella.

University Courtyard
The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library.

Michael hurries to catch up.

>se

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

Michael strolls along after you.

>s

Whateley Bridge
A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water.

The clouds overhead mutter restlessly to themselves.

Michael hurries to catch up.

>s

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets finally open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

"Wow," Michael says, looking the obelisk up and down. "That is, uh . . . that is an impressive monument. I wonder what it's for?"

>x obelisk
The obelisk measures about three feet square at its base, narrowing slightly as it rises a good fifteen feet to a blunt tip. It seems to be a monument of some sort, although you can see no plaque or marker anywhere near it. Dense, twisting hieroglyphs cover all four sides.

Embedded in the stone high up on one side, very near the top, is an iron ring.

>e

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

Michael follows you.

>s

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

A clammy mist hangs in the air, seeping through your clothes and making you shiver.

Michael hurries to catch up, but stops when he sees the mist. He stares at it for some time.

"It's strange," he murmurs, "I feel like I've seen this before." He takes a step forward and is soon standing in the midst of the curling vapours. Suddenly, the mist parts before him and dissolves away into nothing.

Michael hardly seems surprised at all.

Michael heads west, quickening his pace.

>w
The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills.

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it.

The town is a jumbled sprawl of rooftops spread out below you. You can see the university almost directly to the north and, a bit further out, the smokestacks of the paper mill. The solitary lighthouse and surrounding ocean are visible to the northeast, and to the east you can see the shattered steeple of an old church rising above the treetops. Winding through it all is the thin black ribbon of the Miskahawnee, and right in the center lies the little clearing of Town Square. You can just make out the shape of the obelisk from up here.

The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top of the hill.

Michael is here.

Michael goes southeast.

>se

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

It looms before you in the deepening twilight, the fabled Verlac family mansion. Its dark, creaking presence dominates the clearing and, somehow, even though it is not visible through the trees, the entire valley below. Somehow, the shadow of the Verlacs seems to enshroud all of Anchorhead from here.

Attached to the front door is a typewritten notice.

Michael waits impatiently by the front door.

Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning.

>take notice
You pull the notice down and squint at it in the gloom. It's a letter from the Miskahawnee Regional Utilities Company, explaining that, due to wiring difficulties, the electricity will not be installed until next week. No phone service, either.

"Well, that's wonderful news," remarks Michael dryly, reading over your shoulder.

>unlock door
You unlock the front door with the key to the house.

Michael opens the front door.

Michael enters the house.

>s
You close your umbrella as you step inside.

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open.

Stacked in a towering heap in the middle of the room are all your luggage and belongings, which you had sent ahead through a moving company before driving up to Massachusetts. The reality of this move finally slams home as you stare at the jumble of stuff, virtually everything you own and care about, boxed away and piled up in the middle of the floor.

Michael is here.

Night has fallen. A cold, noiseless draft coils through the foyer and up your back. You are momentarily seized with an almost childlike fear: that of being tiny, timid, and alone in a big, dark place.

The feeling passes, though not without a shiver.

Michael stretches his arms and yawns. "Well," he says, "I think I'm going to turn in. There'll be plenty of time to unpack and explore tomorrow. Good night, hon." He kisses you on the cheek. "Don't stay up too late."

Michael goes upstairs.

>close door
You close the front door.

>lock door
You lock the front door.

>u

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>s

Master Bedroom
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

Michael is curled up in bed, fast asleep.

>enter bed
You take off your clothes, leave your things on the floor, crawl into bed next to Michael and pull the covers up to your chin.

>sleep
Almost immediately you fall asleep, and as the wind slithers around the eaves and the house creaks furtive secrets to itself, you drift into troubling dreams . . .

x pants




You are waiting in your bed, with the coverlet up under your chin, waiting for Father to come tuck you in. Footsteps in the hall pause outside your door. You close your eyes; when you open them again Father is standing at the foot of the bed. His eyes are bloodshot, rimmed with red. You wonder if he has been crying. Slowly, he starts to take off his belt.

Only it's not your father after all, it's Michael. He hands you a golden sphere and says:

"I don't know what it opens yet but I have an idea."

Your reflection is like a little doll trapped inside the sphere. Everything is tinged with gold. Your face swells and stretches comically as you stare back at yourself through the curved, distorting surface. Reaching out, your fingers curve back on themselves. The sphere shatters . . .

You are running from the eyes, the terrible red-rimmed eyes, through endless, narrow corridors of wooden slats and crumbling plaster. You stumble through turn after turn, hopelessly lost. There is a small hole in the boards, and peering through it you see Michael in the study, typing diligently away. You scream, you pound on the wall, but he can't hear you. The eyes draw closer. You hurry down a twisting passage until you stop abruptly at a dead end. The walls crumble . . .

You are running through the streets, following the wind, chasing something terribly important, until you reach another dead end, a high brick wall at the center of a labyrinth of twisting lanes. Written across the wall in black spray paint are the words:

He always returns to his blood.

Behind you, you hear the buzzing of a fly. You turn . . .

look in pocket


                                       
                                       
                                       
                                       
                                THE SECOND DAY
                                       
                                       
                     It is hard to prevent the impression
              of a faint, malign odour about the village street,
             as of the massed mould and decay of centuries . . .
               It is always a relief to get clear of the place.
                                       
                              — H. P. Lovecraft,
                             "The Dunwich Horror"
                                       
                                       
                                       
take wallet




You wake up. Steam billows gently by, and you can hear the sound of running water coming from the bathroom.

Master Bedroom (on the four-poster bed)
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

Clouds of steam billow out from the direction of the bathroom.

Your clothes lie in an untidy pile on the floor.

Michael's pants are draped across the foot of the bed.

You can also see your umbrella, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, a typewritten notice, and your trenchcoat here.

Michael is having what sounds like a heated discussion with himself.

>open wallet
(first taking the wallet from the pants)
You take the wallet from the back pocket.

You open the wallet, revealing Michael's faculty card.

>take card
With a nervous glance towards the bathroom, you slip the library card from Michael's wallet.

>put wallet in pants
(Michael's pants)
(first closing the wallet)
You put the wallet back into the pocket of Michael's pants.

>wear clothes
(first reluctantly getting out of bed)
(first taking your clothes)
You put on your clothes.

>wear trenchcoat
(first taking the trenchcoat)
You put on the trenchcoat.

>take keyring
Taken.

>take umbrella
Taken.

For a second there's an odd noise coming from the bathroom.

>take notice
Taken.

You hear Michael singing, as he often does. You can't make out the lyrics. Something by Nick Cave, maybe?

>wait
Time passes.

The shower suddenly stops.

>wait
Time passes.

Michael emerges from the bathroom, toweling his hair.

"Morning, sweetie," he says absently, giving you a kiss on the cheek. He tosses the towel back into the bathroom and starts getting dressed.

>wait
Time passes.

"Listen, I don't actually start work until Monday," says Michael, "so I think I'm going to stay home and get some writing done. If you go out today, see if you can find a newspaper. I want to learn as much as I can about this town."

He looks at you for a moment, and his voice becomes serious. "Hey. I love you. You know that, don't you? I really want us to try and be happy here."

You feel an ugly knot of guilt in the pit of your stomach — two days into your new life and you're already sneaking through his wallet. But there are things you need to find out about this place, and you're not entirely sure you want Michael knowing about it.

Michael goes north.

>e

Bathroom
A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west.

A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub.

>take towel
(slipping the typewritten notice into your pocket to make room, since your hands are now full)
You pick up the bath towel.

>w

Master Bedroom
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

>n

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>w

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face.

>s

Library
Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors leads to the study, while the hall lies north.

On the armchair is Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales by Galarian Academius Deschamps.

You can hear Michael in the next room, typing away.

>w
You open the doors to see Michael at his desk, typing furiously. He looks up at you with a pained expression. "Hey, if you don't mind, I could really use the next hour or so to myself," he says. "I think I'm onto something really good here, and if I don't get it all out I'm going to lose it. Thanks, sweetie."

You tiptoe out, shutting the doors quietly behind you.

>read manuell
An old medieval tome dating back to the 1300s. The cover is adorned with a gilded pentagram.

You open Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales and start reading.

It seems to be a textbook on the theory and practice of magical wards and seals, mystic symbols inscribed on doors and containers in order to keep people and spirits out — or in. It's all very deliberately vague and arcane (to say nothing of having been originally written in medieval French and then translated into some variety of Middle English), and is probably the sort of thing that got many a scholar burned at the stake in those days.

You are surprised to find that a page in this old and probably quite valuable book has been folded down, as though to mark the place. The section marked discusses various methods of opening doors that have been sealed with the image of a pagan god. According to this, speaking aloud the deity's true name will usually break the seal and open the door.

>n

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face.

>n

Child's Bedroom
This must once have been a child's bedroom — the bed is comically small compared to the one in the master bedroom. A little vanity table occupies the opposite wall, and in the corner under the window stands an antique crib. The only exit is to the south.

On the vanity table is a jewelry box.

>take jewelry box
(slipping the bath towel into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the jewelry box. Hardly larger than your hand, made of wood and charmingly decorated with a picture of children dancing around a Maypole on its closed lid. A plaything of one of the later Verlac children, maybe?

>open box
You open the jewelry box, revealing a silver locket.

>take locket
(slipping the jewelry box into your pocket to make room)
You take the silver locket from the jewelry box. The oval-shaped locket is held shut with a tiny clasp.

>open locket
You open the silver locket, revealing a picture of a boy inside.

>x picture
The boy in the picture looks no more than two, maybe years old. He is smiling, but there is something disconcerting about his expression, as though his head were not shaped quite right, or his features placed slightly off-kilter. Perhaps he had Down's Syndrome or some similar type of disability. It's difficult to tell, because the picture has been cropped so closely you can't even see the edges of the boy's face. Only his features fill the tiny frame.

>look under bed
Under the bed you see several long scratches in the wooden floor, as though the bed has been pushed back and forth against the wall.

>pull bed
Bracing yourself, you push the bed away from the wall, revealing a ragged hole in the plaster behind it.

>x hole
It looks like someone broke in the wall with a hammer and pulled chunks of plaster out with their bare hands. In it you see a bundle of soggy pages.

>take pages
(slipping the silver locket into your pocket to make room)
You take the bundle of soggy pages from the ragged hole. This looks like it was once someone's diary. There must have been a leak at some time behind the wall where it was hidden, because most of the pages are water-logged and completely illegible. However, portions of a few entries remain untouched.

>read pages
Carefully, you turn the sodden pages. The handwriting is that of a young girl, and you idly wonder if this might have been the diary of one of Edward Verlac's daughters. But no, you see part of a date, just visible in the corner of one page: 1953. So this was written several years before Edward was born.

Only fragments of a few entries are legible:

" . . . Father came again to my bed last night . . . mother doesn't . . . tells me I can't . . . to be a good daughter. Sometimes it hurts, but Father always tells me I shouldn't cry. Father says a daughter must do her Duty if she wants to get into Heaven. And I do want to get into Heaven . . ."

Another entry, dated 1955:

" . . . my poor little William. Father calls him an aberration, child of the Devil, but I don't believe . . . in the attic, and threw the key off Whateley Bridge. I go to see him whenever Father is away. I sing to him, sometimes, through the keyhole, and slip him sweets through the crack under the door . . . my baby is beautiful . . . hurt my sweet baby William . . . to the doctor, and he has a plan . . . I can never . . . this locket, sweet William, and I will always keep yours . . . to always remember my face . . ."

Nothing else is readable except for one fragmented entry near the very end, dated February 27, 1964:

" . . . dead, but not dead yet . . . will not allow him to do to Edward what he wanted to do to William . . . have learned . . . given him the charm against the . . . never take it off, dear Edward . . ."

A page is torn away, then:

" . . . fear to sleep . . . mist at the window . . ."

And that's all.

