Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face.

>e

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>d

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>e

Dining Room
A huge dining table runs the length of the room, its surface hidden under a thick blanket of dust. Above it, a crystal chandelier twinkles weakly in the gloom. Doorways lead elsewhere to the south and west.

>x chandelier
An elaborate construction of glittering beads and pendants arranged around a cluster of cheap, candle-shaped bulbs. A bit ostentatious, really.

The central pendant is somewhat larger and brighter than the other crystals.

>x pendant
(the central pendant)
It's nearly the size of your fist. There's a weird asymmetry to it that is difficult for you to visually process — it's as though no matter what angle you view it from, the facets don't seem like they should all fit together according to conventional geometry.

>stand on table
You've never resented being short before, but the number of times this town has put you in situations where you have to stand on something in order to reach something important is getting a little exasperating. With a short sigh, you clamber onto the table.

>take pendant
(the central pendant)
(slipping the bundle of soggy pages into your pocket to make room)
The crystal snaps free and falls into your hand, like ripe fruit from a tree branch.

>x pendant
(the asymmetrical crystal)
It's nearly the size of your fist, and quite heavy. There's a weird asymmetry to it that is difficult for you to visually process — it's as though no matter what angle you view it from, the facets don't seem like they should all fit together according to conventional geometry.

>get off table
You hop down.

>w

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>w

Sitting Room
A sparse but functional parlor. An old, colonial rug covers the floor, and the sofa is arranged so that visitors can enjoy the warmth of the large, ornate fireplace that fills the southern wall. Rooms lie to the east and south.

On the antique sofa is a scrapbook.

From above the fireplace, a massive portrait glares down at you with patrician contempt.

>x portrait
(the founder's portrait)
The man in the portrait is the very epitome of Puritan patriarchy, from the tarnished buckle of his hat to the grimly knotted bows of his garters. Though his outward pose seems dignified, you think you detect the unsettling glow of fanaticism in his haggard, red-rimmed eyes: a hint of something cruel, possibly even insane. If this was the original founder of the Verlac clan, perhaps it's no wonder they all ended up murdered or mad.

>take scrapbook
(slipping the asymmetrical crystal into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the scrapbook. It's just a cheap scrapbook album, the kind you can get in any craft store. The first dozen or more pages are pasted over with yellowed documents and newspaper clippings.

>x scrapbook
It's just a cheap scrapbook album, the kind you can get in any craft store. The first dozen or more pages are pasted over with yellowed documents and newspaper clippings.

>open scrapbook
Among the clippings you find:

  - a reproduction of a woodcut illustration
  - a photostatic copy
  - an article dated 1919
  - an article dated 1935
  - an article dated 1952
  - an article dated 1956
  - a mimeographed bulletin
  - an obituary
  - an article dated 1960
  - an article dated 1973
  - an article dated 1988
  - a crumpled flyer
  - a wedding announcement

>x woodcut
(a reproduction of an antique woodcut illustration)

The old, sepia-toned illustration depicts some sort of astrological event in medieval times. A comet hangs darkly in the sky, its tail reaching down towards the earth like a cluster of grasping tendrils. Buildings crumble and burn in the town below. Townsfolk scurry and clutch their heads in despair . . . or are they dancing? Shouting and reveling in joy? The more you look, the harder it is to tell.

>x photostatic copy
(a photostatic copy of a much older document; a note in the margin dates it to the end of the 18th century)

    BE IT KNOWNE that, through the Generoſitie and Enterpriſe
of the Hon. Goodman Heinrich Verlac, the Miſkahawnee Valley
Mille shall be Rebuilt, even upon the Ashes of the Olde which
was Tragickally Burnt; and

    BE IT KNOWNE that, upon its Newe Christening, there shall
Ariſe a Sore Need for Men of Able Bodie and Induſtrious Bent
to Work therein; and

    BE IT KNOWNE that the Hon. Goodman Heinrich Verlac is
thereby holding a General Call for Employe, and any Honeſt
Man of Working Age is Encouraged to Applye his Self thereto.

