historic paper mill  to the ground
despite their best efforts.
    The mill,  which provides jobs
for  roughly   two-thirds  of  the
town's  population,   caught  fire
yesterday around 4:00 pm. Although
there  have been rumors  of an ex-
plosion,  the  exact cause  of the
fire remains unknown.
    Fire  Inspector  Donald  Brown
remains  puzzled.   "We're  seeing
evidence of chemical  scoring that
is inconsistent with the materials
used  in  paper  manufacture,"  he
told reporters. "The heat involved
must  have been  intense.  There's
metal fused  with metal  in there.
It's all messed up."
    Interestingly, this is not the
first time the Anchorhead mill has
burned.   The  original  mill  was
built  by  Wilhelm  Verlac  in the
latter  part of  the 17th century,
and  historical  records  indicate
that it burned down after only ten
years.  The  mill  was  rebuilt by
Wilhelm's grandson,  Heinrich Ver-
lac,  a  few  decades  later. This
one lasted until 1862, when it was
burned down  yet again,  some his-
torians claim  by Confederate sym-
pathizers.  The  current  mill was
rebuilt yet again in 1922 by Hein-
rich's  great-great-grandson,  the
Reverend Mordecai Verlac.  In add-
ition  to  being a  fiery preacher
and outspoken Prohibitionist, Mor-
decai  Verlac  was  quite wealthy,
with numerous and  widespread  in-
vestments. At the age of 70,  this
