The announcement is dated December 12, 1984, and is accompanied by a picture of the young couple. She has long, dark hair and a serious expression; he, although only in his twenties, is already starting to bald a little. You find yourself unable to tear your eyes away from his face for some time. It seems open and guileless, and he is clearly very happy. There is no hint there of the nightmare his world would one day become.

>s

Gallery
A long room paneled in dark wood, with narrow windows at either end letting in little natural light. Paintings line one wall, mounted beneath small, shaded lamps that would illuminate the canvasses nicely if only the electricity were working.

For what must be the hundredth time since you got up this morning, you wish you could open the windows in this place.

Doorways lead north and east.

>x paintings
There are four portraits hanging on the wall, each depicting one of the Verlacs' storied ancestors: Wilhelm, Heinrich, Elijah, and Mordecai.

>x wilhelm
This man's costume, with its coat, lace cravat, and towering wig spilling powdered ringlets across his shoulders, places him squarely in the 17th century. His lips are curled in an arrogant sneer, but it is his eyes that that carry the true family resemblance, edged with the same raw madness that haunts the red-tinged gaze of the founder's portrait that hangs in the sitting room.

Standing behind the man, resting one pale hand on his shoulder, is a woman, older by about twenty years. Unlike him, she wears no wig. Her hair is a shining curtain of jet black hanging well past her shoulders. She bears a strong family resemblance, but not about the eyes. Her eyes are not red at all. They are black and cold and very, very deep.

The plaque beneath the painting reads Wilhelm Verlac. It makes no mention of the woman at all.

>x heinrich
The subject of this portrait seems to be in his late 50s. A big man, solidly built and healthy, he is dressed in a tailored coat and breeches fashionable in the late 1700s, less severe than the clothes of his ancestor in the sitting room, but still quite formal and dignified. His hair, dark and thick despite his apparent age, is pulled back and tied at the nape of his neck with a black ribbon. He stares out at the viewer with dour, contemptuous pride, and perhaps some glimmer of the madness that stains the gaze of his forefathers.

In his left hand he holds what you at first take to be a draftsman's compass . . . but as you look closer you're not so sure. It appears to be a drafting instrument of some kind, but its strangely flowing, almost organic curves seem designed to capture grotesque, asymmetrical angles not found in any earthly architecture.

The plaque beneath the painting reads Heinrich Verlac.

>x elijah
The portrait depicts a man who is at least in his 70s: shrunken and deeply scored by time, his skin mottled with liver spots, his gray hair swept back from a high, bony forehead. It's difficult to place him in any particular era, because instead of wearing normal clothing he is draped in some sort of ceremonial robe, its yellow and scarlet silks billowing around his bony frame. Unlike the subjects of the other portraits, he does not stare directly out at the viewer, but rather gazes into the depths of something above and beyond the painting's frame, a darkness that is reflected in the bloodshot madness that seethes within the hollow caves of his skull.

In his right hand the man holds some sort of flute, a strangely twisted instrument with irregularly spaced holes spiraling down its length. Looking closely at it, you notice another odd detail about him: he appears to have an extra digit, a sixth finger, on his right hand.

The plaque beneath the painting reads Elijah Verlac.

>x mordecai
The man in this portrait has returned to his Puritan roots with a vengeance: dressed in a spartan, black cassock, hair clipped in an archaic tonsure, clutching a Bible to his chest (you assume it's a Bible; you can't actually make out the words on its cover) as he glares out at the viewer with censorious rage; the look of a man who would whip his children for laughing on Sunday. More than any of the others he resembles the man in the portrait over the sitting room fireplace. His eyes blaze with that unmistakable Verlac madness, rimmed with red and burning with single-minded righteousness.

Standing in front and just to the side of the man is a young girl, not more than ten or twelve years old. She wears a simple frock dress that would have been modest even by 1940s standards, and her eyes are slightly downcast. Her father's hand rests on her shoulder. It weighs her down like a shackle.

The plaque beneath the painting reads Mordecai Verlac. It makes no mention of the girl at all.

It's strange . . . the portraits are so vivid, each capturing the personality of its subject with such meticulous detail, it seems obvious that they were painted from life. However, you are equally certain that they were all painted by the same artist. Not that you're any kind of expert, but seeing them all next to each other, the similarities in style and technique seem obvious.

But these paintings are spread across multiple generations. Some of these people lived centuries apart. So both of those things cannot possibly be true.

>e

Back Hall
A short hallway, connecting rooms to the east, west and north.

>e

Kitchen
The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house.

Someone's flashlight is lying on the kitchen counter; one of the movers must have left it.

An odd feeling of gloom overtakes you momentarily, and you find yourself thinking about the Verlac family's personal effects — the everyday mundanities, the spare lightbulbs, the kitchen utensils. Were they all thrown out? Donated to charity? Auctioned off as grisly souvenirs? For a morbid minute or two, you are fascinated by the question of what Mrs. Edward Verlac might have been cooking for dinner, the night her husband blew her head off. What about canned goods, did anyone think to take them? Could they still be in the cabinets?

>take flashlight
(slipping the scrapbook into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the flashlight. It's the kind where the top swivels, so you can put it in your pocket and it will still shine out and illuminate your way. Looks waterproof, too. It is switched off.

>open cabinets
No canned goods, thank goodness. There's nothing in the cabinets but an old book of matches.

>take matches
(slipping the flashlight into your pocket to make room)
You take the book of matches from the cabinets. The label on the side reads, "Water-resistant matches. Strike anywhere." There are five matches left.

>e

Pantry
The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back presumably leads down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west.

Leaning in the corner is an old broom.

>take broom
(slipping the book of matches into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the broom.

>d
You unlock the cellar door with the key to the cellar.

You open the cellar door.

You descend the steps into the dank, cave-like cellar.

Darkness
It is pitch dark, and you can't see a thing.

>turn on flashlight
The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam.

Cellar
Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor.

A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall.

>n

Storage Room
Old crates and boxes piled high against the walls make this room seem even smaller and dingier than it actually is, which is saying a lot. A doorway lies south; the other walls contain nothing but shadows and dirt-filled corners.

>x crates
The accumulated minutiae of almost four centuries' worth of one family's strangeness. Most of it seems to be junk — old linens, dusty knick-knacks, exciting stuff like that. On the other hand, you might find something interesting if you took the time to search through it.

>search crates
You spend some time poking dispiritedly through boxes of second-hand table settings, discarded shoes, and outdated encyclopedias, but the most interesting things you manage to turn up are an old fuse and a big, nasty-looking spider web in a corner behind one of the crates.

>take fuse
(slipping the umbrella into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the old fuse. It's a big round plug made of porcelain and glass, scratched and dirty, although the little metal strip inside still looks intact.

>wipe web with broom
With a single sweep of the broom, you clear the spider web away, revealing an old-fashioned iron key lying in the corner.

>take iron key
(slipping the old fuse into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the old-fashioned iron key. It's an antique key, the kind with a long shaft and a big, square bit at the end.

>s

Cellar
Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor.

A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall.

>w

Wine Cellar
This wing of the cellar looks like it was part of an earlier structure, older than the rest of the house, with rough walls made of unmortared stone. Tall, heavy wine racks lean in on all sides — all of them empty. An exit to the east leads back to the main cellar.

You are reminded of something Michael told you during the long drive up: about how when the police arrived after the shooting, they found the wine cellar in a shambles. Every bottle thrown to the floor and shattered, the whole cellar reeking of rotted grapes. Every single bottle. No one could posit a motive for it.

>drop broom
Dropped.

>x racks
The racks must have at one time held hundreds and hundreds of bottles; now the notched shelves are empty.

Behind one of the racks, you can make out some markings on the wall.

>pull rack
You manage to drag the wine rack over to one side, revealing a mural covering the north wall.

>x markings
The entire north wall is covered with a bas-relief mural depicting . . . you're not sure what, exactly. Groups of raised bumps scattered randomly here and there, intersecting lines and arcs dividing the space into irregularly shaped sections, circles of various sizes.

>x mural
The entire north wall is covered with a bas-relief mural depicting . . . you're not sure what, exactly. Groups of raised bumps scattered randomly here and there, intersecting lines and arcs dividing the space into irregularly shaped sections, circles of various sizes.

>e

Cellar
Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor.

A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall.

>u

Pantry
The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back stands open, revealing dark stairs leading down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west.

>w

Kitchen
The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house.

>s
You unlock the back door with the key to the house.

You open the back door.

Overgrown Path
A gravel path, nearly overgrown with weeds and briars, starts at the back door of the house and disappears into the undergrowth to the southeast, where the crooked trees seem to form a stunted, jagged archway into the dim recesses of the forest.

The back door stands open.

>se
Stooping to avoid the sharp, bare twigs that snag your clothes and seem to reach greedily for your eyes, you pick your way through the undergrowth to a small clearing.

Family Plot
Moss-slimed tombstones, many of them broken off and leaning crazily like an old man's teeth, poke up from the soft, mulchy earth. The trees press close around this quiet enclave of death, leaning together over your head as if sharing secrets. A gap in the undergrowth provides an exit to the northwest.

In the middle of the clearing stands a crumbling mausoleum, its heavy iron door nearly obscured by thick draperies of ivy.

>x tombstones
Years of neglect have left the tombstones in bad repair. The ones not broken are eroded to near-illegibility, and those not eroded are covered with thick, sticky moss. Most of them must be servants' graves, though, since family members would have been interred in the mausoleum.

>enter mausoleum
You unlock the iron door with the old-fashioned iron key.

You open the iron door.

The musty smell of decay grows stronger as you descend.

In the Crypt
The air is frigid down here. The walls are damp and streaked with mud and lichen and worse things, and pale, swollen roots push through cracks in the masonry. The smell of corruption is almost overpowering. Stairs to the north lead back up to the surface.

The walls of this chamber contain dozens of wide, shallow niches; in each niche, a coffin.

One in particular catches your eye: a child's coffin, tiny and built from cheap pine.

>x coffin
(the child's coffin)
Unlike the others, the child's coffin is a ramshackle box of cheap pine, held together with rusting nails. It's half-rotten with age and so shabbily built you could probably open it with your bare hands.

>open coffin
(the child's coffin)
Compelled by a ghoulish curiosity, you dig your fingers under the soft, rotting boards and pry up the lid. With the shriek of pulling nails and a shower of crumbling wood, the coffin comes open, and you look inside, expecting to find a child's tiny skeleton . . .

Dear God. There's no child buried here. Instead, the coffin contains only the worm-eaten bones of some kind of animal.

Who would have buried an animal in place of the child that should have been interred here? Why would they do such a thing? You look around at the rest of the coffins, and glumly realize that anyone who might have known the answers to these questions is probably already buried in this room.

>x bones
These cannot possibly be the bones of a human child. A dog, maybe, if you had to guess.

>take bones
The skeleton is in pieces; parts of it crumble even as you touch it. Only the animal's skull seems reasonably intact.

>take skull
You pick up the dog's skull. The gruesome thing seems to leer at you with its bleached, toothy grin.

>n
Gratefully, you leave the noisome clamminess of the crypt behind.

Family Plot
Moss-slimed tombstones, many of them broken off and leaning crazily like an old man's teeth, poke up from the soft, mulchy earth. The trees press close around this quiet enclave of death, leaning together over your head as if sharing secrets. A gap in the undergrowth provides an exit to the northwest.

The iron door of the mausoleum stands open, revealing steps leading down into subterranean gloom.

>nw

Overgrown Path
A gravel path, nearly overgrown with weeds and briars, starts at the back door of the house and disappears into the undergrowth to the southeast, where the crooked trees seem to form a stunted, jagged archway into the dim recesses of the forest.

The back door stands open.

>n

Kitchen
The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house.

The back door stands open.

>turn off flashlight
You switch the flashlight off.

>lock back door
You close the back door.

You lock the back door.

>w

Back Hall
A short hallway, connecting rooms to the east, west and north.

>n

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>n
You unlock the front door with the key to the house.

You open the front door.

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open.

>close door
You close the front door.

>nw

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top.

>e

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind.

>n

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>w

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

A damp newspaper lies on the curb, fluttering slightly in the wind.

>take newspaper
(the Innswich Herald)
(slipping the dog's skull into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the newspaper. It's the Weekly Innswich Herald. Anchorhead, apparently, is not large enough to warrant its own newspaper.

>read newspaper
(the Innswich Herald)
The front page story is about Jeffrey Greer, 8 years old, who was abducted from his home at #38 Mill Town Road last night. Little Jeffrey is the latest victim in a series of abductions that stretches back for years, and that authorities believe is the work of a single perpetrator. Early investigations led some to suspect Edward Verlac, Anchorhead resident and professor of history at Miskahawnee University, but local police were unable to obtain a confession or any hard proof. Verlac had been convicted of murdering his wife and two daughters in January of this year; he was found not guilty by reason of insanity and incarcerated in Dalmers Asylum, where he remained until committing suicide last March. This latest kidnapping, occurring months after Edward's death, seems to have cleared up any lingering suspicions that he might have been the culprit. Anyone possessing information regarding the whereabouts of Jeffrey Greer is strongly urged to speak to the authorities as soon as possible.

There's also a weather advisory down in the lower corner of the page: heavy offshore fog today and tomorrow. Boaters and fishermen are urged to stay on shore; if you must go out on the water, navigate by the buoys and make sure to set a beacon before casting off.

>s
You climb the short flight of steps and enter the courthouse.

Courthouse
A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north.

>s

Records Department
A single bulb dangling from the ceiling casts its dirty dishwater light over a wasteland of papers, thousands of records and documents stacked everywhere in tottering piles. Stairs lead back up to ground level.

>x records
The archives contain centuries' worth of records, most yellow and brittle with age. Documents are crammed into shelves, stuffed into bulging cardboard boxes, tied up with twine like bales of hay and stacked higher than your head.

>look up croseus
The only thing you find under that name is a very old certificate of death, dated December 3, 1651. No official cause of death is given, but then this would have been before such data were officially recorded.

>look up wilhelm
Fortunately you are able to find, buried under a massive pile of fishing license extension applications, a general index that lists records by name. After lots of digging through the archives, you finally manage to track down the records for Wilhelm Verlac. Born December 3, 1651; died August 25, 1734.

>look up heinrich
After quite a bit of sorting, you finally manage to pull up the records for Heinrich Verlac. Born August 25, 1734; died March 18, 1802.

>look up elijah
After a great deal of searching through a few of the more structurally stable piles, you finally manage to uncover the records for Elijah Verlac. Born March 18, 1802; died February 6, 1886.

>look up mordecai
You manage to track down the records for Mordecai Verlac. Born February 6, 1886; died August 30, 1959. The death certificate lists the official cause of death as "natural causes."

>look up edward
After a fair amount of hunting, you manage to find the records for Edward Verlac. Born August 30, 1959; died March 14, 1997. The death certificate lists the cause of death as "suicide."

>look up william
After hours of searching, you finally manage to track down the records for William Verlac. Born June 22, 1954; died September 18, 1956. The death certificate lists the official cause of death as "failure to thrive." It looks like it was scribbled hastily, in a different handwriting than the rest of the information on the form.

>n

Courthouse
A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north.

