University Courtyard
The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library.

>w

Reading Room
Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk.

>n

Circulation Desk
A high counter divides the reading area from the stacks. A small sign affixed to the wall next to the counter provides instructions for those seeking to check out books.

On the counter are a service bell and a dog-eared register.

>read sign
It reads, "Please be prepared to show your faculty ID to the librarian. Books may not be removed from the library."

>read register
It's a log, recording what book was checked out, who checked it out, and when. There are hundreds of entries in this old thing, too many to simply read through. You'd do better to look up a specific name.

>look up michael
Your husband's name is right on the first page. He checked out A Historical Overview of Superstitions by Arnsworth Frazer.

>ring bell

Ding.

A moment later, a gaunt and fishy-eyed librarian silently emerges from the shadows behind the counter.

>show card to librarian
The librarian looks the card over, nods slowly, and hands it back to you without a word.

>ask librarian for superstitions
Wordlessly the librarian retreats back into the shadows, only to reappear the next moment carrying the thick, dusty tome you saw your husband with earlier. Dutifully, you sign the register, and the librarian sets the book on the counter before disappearing again.

>take superstitions
(slipping the flask into your pocket to make room)
You pick up A Historical Overview of Superstitions. A thick, academic tome, entitled A Historical Overview of Superstitions in the Miskahawnee Valley Region: Pre-Colonial Era to Present, by Arnsworth Frazer.

>read superstitions
As you open the book, a slip of paper falls from its pages and flutters to the ground.

>take slip
(slipping A Historical Overview of Superstitions into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the slip of paper. Someone was apparently using it as a bookmark. There's some writing on one side.

>read slip
It says:

  —wine cellar
  —proces. of stars?
  —born-died same date?
  —chk. court. records
— chk. if lib. has Monmouth book

The handwriting is unmistakably Michael's.

>read superstitions
You open A Historical Overview of Superstitions and start reading.

The book covers quite a lot of information, much of it dull and difficult to follow. A glance at the table of contents reveals a few interesting topics, however:

  - Introduction
  - The Legend of Croseus Verlac
  - Rituals of the Misquahaw Indians
  - The Dark Man and Other Aspects
  - The Strangling Mist
  - The Ghost Train

>read introduction
The introduction begins:

New England is an abundant storehouse of American myth and folklore, and the Miskahawnee River Valley in particular has long been recognized as particularly fecund ground for tall tales and fanciful superstition. Pagan rituals enacted by degenerate, hill-dwelling races; malevolent entities offering Faustian pacts to the foolish and hubris-laden; indescribable grotesqueries squirming up from the depths of our subconscious — these are the dark, untilled subsoil from which our more traditional stories of ghosts, goblins and witches draw their original inspiration. In collecting and cataloguing these myths, this volume attempts to trace their evolution, to lay bare the hidden relationship between our modern sensibilities and their dark roots — in short, to understand the modern mind by explicating the mind of the past.

 . . . it goes on like that, bombastic and dry. It was published in 1916 by Miskahawnee University Press, and the preface notes that Frazer was a professor there at the time.

>read legend
This chapter deals with the strange mythology surrounding the person of Croseus Verlac, first of the American Verlacs, who immigrated from the Black Forest region of Germany in the early 1600s.

His origins are shrouded in mystery; records hint that he came from a wealthy family, and there are obscure references to his being born "under the auspice of the Many-Angled Gate." He settled in the Miskahawnee Valley and there helped establish the small fishing port that would eventually be known as Anchorhead.

Though Croseus quickly became a powerful and influential figure in the region, he was also singularly aloof and often the subject of gossip. His six raven-haired daughters, all schooled at home, were reclusive and odd of habit, shunned by locals who called them "the Old Man's Coven" when conveniently beyond earshot. By the time the eldest turned fifteen, the town had all but openly accused them of witchcraft.

No records survive of anyone who might be identified as Croseus Verlac's wife, and contemporary accounts suggest that he may not have had one. He allegedly sired a number of grandsons by more than one of his daughters, ostensibly to keep the Verlac blood "pure," such practice unfortunately being far from uncommon in the more secluded and xenophobic settlements of the time. However, the offspring were all stillborn, or horribly deformed, or both, and there were furtive whispers that Croseus was practicing some form of dark sorcery upon his progeny. On the day that his first healthy child was born to his youngest daughter Eustacia, Croseus fell ill and died before day's end.

After Croseus's death, the townspeople turned against his daughters, burning all of them at the stake except for Eustacia, who managed to escape along with her infant son, Wilhelm. She returned some years later, after an outbreak of smallpox had wiped out much of the town's older population, including the Calvinist minister and every last man and woman who had participated in the burning of Verlac's brood.

>read rituals
Little is known about the enigmatic Misquahaw tribe. They are believed to have occupied a small, unobtrusive territory around southeastern Massachusetts, along the banks of the river which now bears their name. At the time of this book's publication, property disputes prevent a thorough archaeological investigation of the area; information regarding this tiny, sequestered tribe is therefore scarce and based largely on hearsay and folklore.

Analysis of the only known fossil — a partial skull — has led some anthropologists to conclude that the Misquahaw were not indigenous to the region. A once popular theory held that the tribe was most closely related to certain "degenerate" branches of the Canadian Inuit (Frazer uses the term "Esquimaux"), and were perhaps driven from their original sub-arctic clime and forced to settle in exile in what would become the northeastern United States.

Although precious few physical artifacts have been recovered — chief among them a pair of ritual masks and a crudely carved, seven-holed wind instrument — tall tales of Misquahaw ritual abound. They appear to have been a unilaterally reviled tribe. Most of the whispered stories involve horrid, ululating chants around blazing bonfires in the dead of winter night, grotesque copulations performed in honor of bestial gods, and of course abundant human sacrifice. The Misquahaw were widely believed to be child-stealers, creeping through open windows at night to perpetrate foul kidnappings. None of these claims can be verified, of course, and scholars are quick to point out that nearly all Native American tribes encountered by the first European settlers have been subject to such prejudices at one time or another.

It is known that the Misquahaw were star-worshippers, and possessed a quite complex theology involving a unique zodiacal system and entities that dwelled beyond "the bowl of tiny fires," their term for the night sky. These entities granted wisdom or insanity, bestowed prosperity or famine, according to how well or how laxly the tribe performed its ritual appeasements. The rituals attempted to contact or possibly summon aspects of these entities through elaborately carved "beacons," stone mounds or obelisks placed at significant geographical locations.

The Misquahaw were one of the few North American tribes to have developed a system of writing prior to any contact with Europeans, and the few eroded hieroglyphs left by them have proved a compelling but so far intractable puzzle for linguists today; any further detail about their ritual and mythology remains yet a mystery.

>read dark man
Deeply religious and at the same time almost hysterically superstitious, clinging precariously to the edge of an unexplored and terrifying continent, it was only natural for people in the early American settlements to project their collective fears onto the unknown. For the predominantly fundamentalist Protestant sects that first colonized the New World, these projections typically invoked a unique variation on the Christian concept of the Devil — a figure commonly known to folklorists today as "The Dark Man."

The Dark Man generally takes the form of a human male, sometimes of large or even giant stature but more often no larger than an ordinary man. He is invariably dark-skinned, but perhaps surprisingly is only rarely described as a Negro. Rather, he is most often pictured with Caucasian features, but anomalously jet-black skin, thus combining the refined, cunning intelligence of the European with the base carnality of the African. He is regularly portrayed as the consort of witches. He has many names: The Dark Man, The Grinning Man, Old Scratch, Crowhead, Springheel Jack, etc., but always his formal, Biblical appellation — "Lucifer" or "Satan" — is scrupulously avoided, a holdover from the tradition that to speak a demon's name is to attract its attention and perhaps even summon it.

More interesting to the folklorist are the names that harken further back than these simple Christian superstitions, recalling a more pagan portrayal of the dark and unknown. These tales, which inevitably originate from settlements of singularly remote and reclusive aspect, often bring out the more animalistic, nature-worshipping aspect of the Dark Man. He is sometimes pictured as being covered with hair, or having hooves instead of feet, resembling classical images of Pan. His names are more obscure: The Wicker (or Wicca) Man; The King in Tatters; The Black Goat with a Thousand Young. Therein lie tantalizing clues offering the enterprising folklorist still deeper glimpses into the collective unconscious.

A handful of references have been uncovered that point beyond even these antiquated myth-symbols, bizarre aspects that seem to reflect some of the less understood concepts of Native American mysticism. Such baroque names as "The Lurker at the Threshold" or "The Herald of the Many-Angled Gate" point to a substratum of human mythology as yet untouched. These versions typically describe not physical manifestations, but rather abstract concepts of Space and Time that some scholars have linked to the pre-Roman god Saturn, before he became characterized as merely the father of Zeus, when he was instead identified with the Ouroboros Dragon, Devourer of Worlds. Hopefully, as more archaeological evidence is uncovered, scholars will be able to speak of these primordial connections with greater confidence.

>read mist
Unique to the lower Miskahawnee River Valley, this tale centers around a seemingly malevolent fog that roams the forests and lonely night roads, choking the unwary traveler with invisible, untouchable hands.

The experience of being attacked by this strange entity is vividly described in an 18th-century traveler's journal: " . . . lyke as thowe a deade man were to put his corpsey fingers downe yr throate withe one hande, & up yr nostrille withe the other . . ."

No two tellings can agree on the origins of this terrible mist. Some accounts insist that it is a spirit of the restless dead; others attribute the effect to malicious hobgoblins. Other versions implicate witchcraft, a pirate's curse, swamp faerie . . . the list goes on. Some of the more esoteric explanations seem to indicate that the legend was adapted by white settlers from native superstitions held by tribes indigenous to the Miskahawnee region; however, there is no evidence as yet that the "strangling mist" existed in any form prior to the appearance of Europeans.

>read ghost train
Yet another colorful folk legend involves the recurring image of a "ghost train" — a spectral locomotive that materializes from nowhere, glides across the haunted track for a short period of time, and then disappears as mysteriously as it came.

This story did not originate among the first white settlers, of course; it only came into being after the advent of the steam locomotive in our burgeoning Machine Age. The earliest known recorded version of this story, in fact, is dated 1882. Nonetheless, the legend provides us with an interesting example of how the collective unconscious adapts itself to changing aspects of our culture, cloaking old symbolism in the trappings of new technology.

Although many versions hold that the ghost train represents the ghastly echoes of a locomotive that was wrecked (derailed and lost off a mountain pass being the most popular means of destruction), this is in fact a simplistic interpretation not seen until many decades after the myth originated. More intriguing and more useful to the folklorist are versions that explain the ghost train as a transport to the land of the dead — a modernized boat of Charon, ferrying damned souls across the shroud to the devil's newly industrialized Hell. These are the versions which most faithfully maintain links to the traditions of the past, and demonstrate the curious evolutionary behavior of the myth.

In some of these tellings, the traveling soul must have a ticket to present to the grim conductor, a trope directly analogous to the ancient custom of placing of gold coins on the eyes or beneath the tongue of the deceased in order to buy passage to the underworld. In other versions, living souls who ventured too near the tracks as the ghost train made its nightly sojourn would find themselves swept along, echoing the Germano-Celtic myth of the Wild Hunt, in which witness were compelled to join as either hunter or prey. Those who board the train, whether by accident, madness, or foolishness, are inevitably carried back to whatever eldritch dimension from whence the train originated. Tales of return voyages are rare, and generally held by those who pass them along to be apocryphal.

