You pick up Stranger Stars. The cover of this book depicts a medieval scholar looking up at the night sky. Several of the stars are arranged in a pattern that suggests an eye, looking back down at him; for some weird reason you find yourself instinctively repulsed by the image.

>read stranger stars
You open Stranger Stars and start reading.

The book contains an overview of zodiacal systems and the mythologies that surround them across different cultures and periods of history. Various chapters go into more specific detail regarding Western versus Eastern astrologies, sidereal versus tropical zodiac systems, zodiac systems based on cycles other than the lunar calendar, and so on.

One chapter near the end of the book catches your eye:

Of particular interest — and no little controversy — amongst practitioners and scholars of astrology both are the so-called "black" zodiac systems that are periodically discovered in various parts of the globe. Such systems are notorious for displaying little to no similarity whatsoever to the myth-symbols of the prevailing cultures in the regions in which they appear, while at the same time corresponding very closely with each other, despite invariably occurring in tiny, geographically isolated areas, and often separated in history by centuries or more. These remarkable coincidences have prompted many to dismiss the entire notion of the "black zodiac" as a persistent hoax, perpetrated by conspiracy theorists, religious cultists, or fifth columnists seeking to discredit astrology altogether.

One property shared by most black zodiacs is an inability to map neatly onto any known calendrical system. According to some theories, this is because the symbols are based on constellations formed by stars viewed from a point in space other than Earth. Regardless of the truth of such claims, determining the sign under which a particular date falls often involves a fair amount of guesswork, as no two black zodiac systems precisely agree. Table 16-1 lists one possible system, based on the symbols and date ranges most commonly attributed to certain New England witch-cults in the 17th and 18th centuries:

the Mask                       January 16 - February 15
the Black Herald               February 16 - March 19
the Spiraling Worm             March 20 - April 16
the Priest                     April 17 - May 17
the Crawling Flame             May 18 - June 21
the Formless Mother            June 22 - July 19
the Gnawer of Graves           July 20 - August 10
the Toad                       August 11 - September 14
the Piper                      September 15 - October 10
the King in Tatters            October 11 - November 15
the Many-Angled Gate           November 16 - December 17
the Sleeper Under the Waters   December 18 - January 15

Occasionally rumors circulate of an additional, thirteenth sign, which may not be named and does not correspond to any date. What precisely it would mean to be born "under" this sign must remain a matter of pure conjecture.

>drop stranger stars
Dropped.

