>ne

Hidden Courtyard
This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the southwest leads back into the labyrinth.

>s
A silvery bell jingles as you open the door.

Curiosity Shop
A warm and pleasantly dim light suffuses this cozy little shop. The shelves are crammed with odd knick-knacks and peculiar merchandise. In the rear of the store, a lit display case contains several items of interest. The courtyard lies outside, to the north.

On the display case is a violin.

The proprietor watches you quietly from behind the display case.

>x proprietor
A fabulously thick and bushy tangle of moustache, beard and eyebrows frame the portly old proprietor's deeply seamed face. His eyes are kind, however, and his smile warm and ingenuous, and he seems content to watch you quietly with his hands folded across his considerable paunch as you browse through his shop.

>x display case
It's an ordinary glass-fronted display case. Lined up inside are a velvet cloth, a tarot deck, a geode, and an amulet. Sitting on top is a violin.

>x amulet
It's an unassuming silver pendant dangling from a thin leather cord, fashioned in the shape of a tiny, upturned hand with a triangle inscribed within the palm. It doesn't look like the fancy, New Age charms on the other shelves. It reminds you a little of one of those cast pewter game pieces from Monopoly.

>ask proprietor about amulet
"Well now, there's a fancy little thing," remarks the proprietor. "A simple charm against evil. A ward against the Eye, you might say. Young chap brought that in — ten, fifteen years ago, I suppose. Said he had no more use for superstitious baubles."

The proprietor reaches in through the back of the case and takes out the amulet. It gleams softly as he holds it up to the light.

"I think, perhaps, that he eventually found different. And I think, perhaps, that you will, too." He leans forward and, before you can react, slips the leather cord around your neck. "But you won't make the same mistake, will you, Miss? No. You go on and keep that, now. My treat. You may find it useful soon."

>n
"Be safe," says the proprietor.

Hidden Courtyard
This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the southwest leads back into the labyrinth.

The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat.

>sw

Twisting Lane
The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction.

A symbol spray-painted on a nearby wall marks a side street leading northeast.

>n
You make your way through the dark, tangled streets, crossing your own path at least twice and retracing your steps after running into a dead end.

It is not long before you are utterly lost. You take several blind turns, hoping to find some landmark you recognize, and suddenly find yourself emerging onto a . . .

Shadowy Corner
The buildings on this side of town are taller, and thus block more of the sky, making this neighborhood a particularly dark and unpleasant one. The main street leads away to the east, and a shadowy driveway leads through a high brick wall to the southwest.

>e

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

>e

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone.

>e

Vacant Lot
Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west.

An old man dressed in dirty rags sits on his mattress, muttering to himself as he stares intently out to sea.

"I WAS A VERY GOOD DOCTOR! DON'T TELL ME ABOUT INFECTION!" bellows the old man.

A gust of wind blows your hair into your face.

>x man
Once this pitiful creature might have been a man of health and dignity, but alcohol and mental illness have dragged him down irretrievably into degenerate squalor. His clothes are filthy tatters; his hair is a matted, knotted mess; the stench of booze and urine surrounds him. He sits on his mattress, seemingly oblivious to you or the rest of the world, and that's where he'll stay until either death or the next drink comes around.

>give flask to man
(first taking the flask from your pocket)
His eyes burning with greed, the old man snatches the flask from your hand, unscrews the cap, and upturns it into his eager mouth. Trickles of pungent brown liquid ooze from the corners of his mouth as he swallows convulsively, his hands trembling, his eyes squeezed shut and streaming tears. Finally, he falls back onto his mattress with a beatific smile on his mud-smeared face, the flask dropping from his fingers.

The wind moans around eaves and over rooftops, as though beset by troubling dreams.

>ask man about himself
The old man sighs, and for a moment he looks and sounds relatively lucid. "Oh, yes, I was a doctor, once. I was a very good doctor. But that was before Anna, you know. Anna and little Edward and —" Suddenly he stops, and will say no more.

>ask man about edward
The old man lets out a vicious cackle. "Oh, they got poor Edward, so they did! Maggots got 'im good. Got into his head, you know. The maggots got into his head, an' they et up the insides of it 'til he had to crack it open to get 'em out. Poor boy." Suddenly he becomes somber. "Poor boy. Delivered him, I did, you know. Pulled him right out of his mama, spanked his little behind. I know, I know. Little Edward and . . . and his . . ." His voice trails off, but you could swear he mouthed the word "brother".

>show locket to man
(first taking the silver locket from your pocket)
The old man recoils at the sight of the picture inside the locket. "Aye, that's him. That's William, so it is," he mutters. "But only a part, y'see. Only the face part. That's not the whole picture."

>ask man about william
For a while the old man simply stares down at his hands, limp and open in his lap. Then, drawing a deep breath, he begins to speak, and for the first time his voice is steady, with no trace of the alcoholic's craving whine.

"Yes, yes, little Edward had a brother, so he did. And I know him. I know of William, so I do. I delivered the child. Anna Verlac was his mother. It . . . it was . . ." He leans over and grips your arm with a hand that is suddenly steady and strong. "It was her father's poison seed, y'see. That evil bastard poisoned her womb and put a monster inside her! What she gave birth to . . . it was . . . it warn't . . . oh, God, when I held that . . . that abortion in the shape of an infant, that thing with its hooves and its writhin' . . . all of its father's evil given flesh . . . except for its eyes. It had its mother's eyes, and its face was all the more dreadful for it." The old man shudders convulsively, and it is a while before he can speak again.

"Anna and I decided between ourselves, we did. The . . . her child . . . it lived for a couple of years, but it was sickly, and in pain, and it could never be normal, nor healthy. She brought it to me . . . I was her doctor, you see, I'd seen her through her child's birth, and she trusted me . . . and I — I put the thing down. Softly, without pain. They laid it to rest in the family crypt.

"And Anna thanked me for it, so she did."

The old man falls silent, and his wavering, bloodshot eyes wander once more out to sea. And he is lost, once again, to the horror and pain of the memories that can only be silenced by drink.

>ask man about anna
The old man looks wistful. "Oh, Anna, she was such a sweet girl, you know. She never deserved it, never deserved it. She came to me when . . ." He falters, and tears begin to stream unchecked down his face. "It shouldn't have happened to the girl. I tried, I was a good doctor, but only fifteen she was, still a child when he . . . when that red-eyed monster took her . . ." His eyes suddenly darken, his fists clench. "HIS OWN DAUGHTER! GOD DAMM THOSE MAGGOTS! SHOULDN'T HAVE DONE THAT TO HIS OWN DAUGHTER! GOD DAMN 'EM ALL!"

A flurry of dead leaves skitters along the ground past you in the wind.

>ask man about crypt
The old man stares at you suspiciously. "William was buried in the family crypt, at his mother's request, so he was. What're you gettin' at? If you think otherwise, you're dead wrong, so y'are."

>show skull to man
(first taking the dog's skull from your pocket)
The old man grows sickly pale. He raises a hand as if to ward off the sight of the skull, to deny the truth one more time; then his hand drops, and he sinks down onto the mattress, groaning.

"Oh, it's true," he cries, "it's true, God help my lying soul. It was her father who wished to kill the thing, not Anna — 'kill it to make way for another,' he said. 'A more fitting vessel.' He wanted to try again — God! The depth of his evil — after the way the first one came out, he wanted to try again!

"Anna came to me and asked for my help, and what could I do? What could I do? It was her child, for God's sake, no matter what else it was, and she couldn't bear to lose it. We took it to the woods, south of town, and left it there, and we weighed the coffin down with the carcass of a dog, to fool her father."

The old man stares at his hands in broken despair. "Then, her father . . . he did what he did again, and that was Edward. Anna was never the same after that, and she died only a few years later. And her William . . . William's still out there, and the blood of every child he's taken is on my hands, because I couldn't bring myself to put him down, all those years ago."

He sits up suddenly, fastening on you with a sick, pleading look.

"I've told you too much, now," he whispers desperately. "They'll know; the maggots'll find out for sure. They'll find me; even in my hole, they'll find me." He lets out an eye-wateringly pungent breath. "P'raps it's best they do. I've spent so long drownin' my sins, p'raps time's come to pay for them at last. This won't do me much good no more," he says, holding up a little copper key. "And yet . . ." He closes his fist around it again. "And yet, maybe there's a way. They say there's ways to protect yourself, wards versus the evil eye and such." The old man stares greedily at the amulet around your neck. "P'raps if I could get me one o' those . . ."

A gust of wind blows your hair into your face.

>give amulet to man
"Thank you kindly, Miss, you're a generous one, so you are," grins the old man as he closes his grimy fingers around the charm. "And here y'are," he says, tossing you the copper key. "I'll pray it does you good."

He staggers to his feet. "I'll be gettin' on, then. If I can make away before nightfall, p'raps I'll be out of their reach before the maggots come out."

He wraps the leather thong around his hand, closing the charm in his fist, and turns to you one last time. "Don't let 'em find you," he says earnestly. "Get out while you can. If you stay, they'll track you down eventually. It's the blood, see. He can smell it, so he can. And he always returns to his blood." And then he shambles off, up the riverwalk towards the town square, his shadow dragging long behind him.

Looking to the sky, you realize that the sun is nearly set. You've been talking to the old man for much longer than you thought, and it will be dark soon.

>w

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>nw
You follow the stone steps down to the edge of the river.

Under the Bridge
Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level.

An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, closed and flush with the surrounding concrete.

A flurry of dead leaves tumbles past you in the wind.

>open manhole
You unlock the manhole cover with the thick copper key.

Woof — it's heavy! But you manage to get your fingers under the edge and pull the cover up with a groan, revealing a set of iron rungs leading down into darkness.

>d
You ease yourself down into the damp, smelly tunnel.

Sewer Tunnel
The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north.

A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling.

>n
The tunnel narrows and descends sharply as you go. Unless your sense of direction is mistaken, you must be crossing the Miskahawnee River — underneath it.

Flooded Passage
Knee-deep water floods this cramped tunnel, dripping constantly from the low ceiling and seeping through thousands of invisible fissures in the walls. There is barely enough room to stand up straight, much less stand somewhere dry. Higher ground lies to the south, while a metal grating blocks the way north.

You notice something shiny fetched up against the base of the grating.

>x object
It's an antique key, the kind with a long shaft and a big, square bit at the end.

>take key
(the old-fashioned brass key)
(slipping the thick copper key and the dog's skull into your pocket to make room)
Taken.

>s

Sewer Tunnel
The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north.

A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling.

>u
You climb out through the opening.

Under the Bridge
Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level.

An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, open to the darkness below.

>se
You take the stairs back up to the street.

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>s

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

>w
The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills.

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top.

>se

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion.

The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone.

>s
You open the front door.

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

You call out to let Michael know you're home, but no one answers. The house is still, hushed. Prickles of unease crawl down your back like centipede legs. Michael isn't here, you realize. The house is empty. You are alone.

>close door
You close the front door.

>u

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>w

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face.

>s

Library
Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors leads to the study, while the hall lies north.

On the armchair is Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales by Galarian Academius Deschamps.

>w
You open the sliding doors.

Study
This room adjoining the library is small and cluttered, though in a comfortable, lived-in sort of way. A writing desk sits under the window, and a large, ornate fireplace fills the northern wall.

Michael's laptop sits in the middle of the writing desk, humming quietly to itself. Sitting next to it is The Cryptical Haermoniacon.

>x laptop
It's a top-of-the-line model: sleek, lightweight, and very fast, a real status symbol for the aspiring writer who wants to look as though he might stop whatever he's doing at the drop of a hat and suddenly hack out a bestselling novel. While you have yet to witness Michael do this, it did make a nice gift to celebrate his new teaching position.

The computer is in standby mode, blank except for a single window requesting a 4-digit user passcode.

>remove ring
(the wedding ring)
You slip the wedding ring from your finger.

>read ring
(the wedding ring)
Engraved in tiny letters along the inside of the ring are your and Michael's initials, and your wedding date — June 28.

>wear ring
(the wedding ring)
The ring fits snugly back onto your finger.

>type 0628 on laptop
The passcode window disappears and the screen suddenly fills with text — the last thing Michael was working on before he got up and left.

>x laptop
It's a top-of-the-line model: sleek, lightweight, and very fast, a real status symbol for the aspiring writer who wants to look as though he might stop whatever he's doing at the drop of a hat and suddenly hack out a bestselling novel. While you have yet to witness Michael do this, it did make a nice gift to celebrate his new teaching position.

Michael has been writing something, but it's not a novel or a story. It looks more like some kind of journal entry.

I cannot sleep.

Or perhaps I should say: I MUST not sleep, since I am perfectly capable of sleep — in fact, lately sleep has come to me more easily, more quickly, and more insidiously than it ever has before. But I don't WANT to sleep.

I was hoping that the terrible dreams would prove to be stress-related, that they would fade once the move was behind us and we'd finally settled in — but that has not turned out to be the case. They're stronger than ever, filled with red-rimmed eyes pursuing me through strange corridors, whispers that tell me I am not who I am. The whispers tell me the secrets of this house, of those who lived here before. They are my blood. They tell me he always returns to his blood. They tell me of secret keys and combinations, processions of dates and stars. My dreams are filled with monsters, all hands and eyes . . . seven hands, one eye . . .six hands . . .three hands, two eyes . . .

I don't know what it opens yet but I have an idea. 

Tomorrow I must go down to the cellar. I must see if the things revealed to me in that terrible book are true.

I know she's worried about me. I want to —

The entry ends there, unfinished.

>close laptop
You close the laptop, and it shuts down automatically.

>read haermoniacon
The full title is The Cryptical Haermoniacon: a Guide to the Atonal Servitors, and no author is listed anywhere on or in the book. Its leather covers are worn and cracked; the metal corner fittings tarnished; the pages yellow and brittle.

You open The Cryptical Haermoniacon and start reading.

The greater portion of the book is written in an incomprehensible, flowing cipher; however, the margins of several pages are glossed in English. The book appears to contain instructions for traveling to somewhere called "the Womb of Nehilim," and either worshipping or summoning (or possibly having sex with? — the text is very unclear) an entity that dwells there: The Watcher Who Is Named Not; nor may that Name be taught among His followers; that Name which may be learned only by gazing directly into His Awful Countenance . . .

The specific details involve a ritual used to open a one-way portal into Nehilim, allowing the magician to enter or, more likely, throw some hapless soul into its chaotic and blasphemous depths. The ritual has something to do with music, and requires the magician to "attune the boundary pillars according to the vibrations of the resonant haermonicae." Whatever that means. The rest, by only the barest contrast, is nothing but pure raving.

>x fireplace
The mantelpiece is carved from dark, polished rosewood and covers the whole wall. It is identical to the downstairs fireplace in most respects, except for the pair of brass spheres mounted on either end.

>x spheres
You see the room behind you in distorted miniature, your own face swelling and stretching comically across the sphere's reflective surface.

Looking closely at one of the spheres, you can make out smudged fingerprints marring the otherwise immaculately polished brass.

>turn sphere
The sphere twists clockwise a few degrees. There is a barely audible click, and the sphere snaps back to its original position. The entire fireplace slides some eighteen inches to the left with a hollow grinding sound, revealing a narrow corridor leading northeast.

>ne

Behind the Walls
Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house.

>e
The passage bends and twists at confusing angles, making it hard to tell which direction you're heading in. You stumble along as best you can.

Behind the Walls
Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house.

A narrow, wooden ladder leads up.

>u

Observatory
Going by the view through the dingy skylight over your head, you'd guess that you are inside the cupola on the roof of the house. The room is tiny and cluttered, with torn and faded astronomical charts covering every wall. A ladder leads down through a hatch in the floor.

In center of the room stands a large mounted telescope, pointed almost straight up through the skylight.

>x charts
The stars on these charts look exactly like those you saw in the mural, down in the wine cellar, though here at least they are clearly labeled. The constellations they form are disturbing and unfamiliar, with pictures of worms and toads and other, less pleasant things drawn along the line of the zodiac. The margins are filled with diagrams and annotations made in crabbed, stilted handwriting. The annotations seem to be calculating the trajectory of one comet in particular, as it gradually approaches the solar system over the course of several centuries. According to these equations, the comet is due to arrive . . .

Well, that's interesting. According to this, the comet is coming tomorrow.

>x telescope
The telescope is an unadorned cylinder made of greenish metal, about eight inches in diameter and nearly four feet long. A thin, rectangular slot is cut into one side, about six inches from the top. Near the bottom, an eyepiece juts out to one side.

>x slot
It's a receptacle of some kind, possibly for housing a special lens, though currently empty.

