Library
Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors opens onto the study, while the hall lies north.

On the armchair is Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales by Galarian Academius Deschamps.

>x books
(the bookshelves)
Literally thousands of books stuff the shelves, most of them thick, cracked, leather-bound tomes with ribbon placeholders and titles lettered in raised gold leaf. It would take you years to peruse them all.

Most of the books are meticulously organized, but one anomaly catches your eye: a copy of Edgar Allen Poe's Tales of Mystery and Imagination, shelved incongruously between volumes "G" and "H" of the Encyclopedia Americana.

>x tales
It's a beautifully bound book in near perfect condition — probably valuable, too, assuming it's authentic. Michael will go nuts when he sees it.

>take tales
The book sticks stubbornly at first; thinking that it must be tightly wedged in between the encyclopediae, you give it a fierce jerk. Suddenly the book slides halfway out, then pops back in with a loud snick.

Amazingly, a section of the bookshelf slides back, revealing a hidden safe.

>set dial to 36
The tumblers tick quietly as you turn the dial to thirty-six.

>set dial to 30
The tumblers tick quietly as you turn the dial to thirty.

>set dial to 17
The tumblers tick quietly as you turn the dial to seventeen, and with a hollow thunk, the safe suddenly swings open, revealing a puzzle box and a strange metal flute.

>take flute
(slipping the old-fashioned brass key into your pocket to make room)
You take the strange metal flute from the safe. It appears to be some kind of wind instrument, fashioned out a weird greenish metal with a greasy finish. It has a slightly curved, twisted shape, so that the finger holes spiral awkwardly around the sides instead of running in a straight line.

>take puzzle box
(slipping the strange metal flute into your pocket to make room)
You take the puzzle box from the safe. It's roughly the size and shape of a cigar box, carved from some dark and oddly streaked wood that you can't identify. All six surfaces are decorated in a complex arrangement of grooves and panels, most of which are illustrated with leering, demonic faces and obscene designs. You can see no obvious way to open it.

