>n

Whateley Bridge
A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water.

The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat.

>n

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

>e

Narrow Street
As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

>s

Twisting Lane
The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction.

A symbol spray-painted on a nearby wall marks a side street leading northeast.

>ne

Hidden Courtyard
This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the southwest leads back into the labyrinth.

>s
"Welcome back," says the proprietor.

Curiosity Shop
A warm and pleasantly dim light suffuses this cozy little shop. The shelves are crammed with odd knick-knacks and peculiar merchandise. In the rear of the store, a lit display case contains several items of interest. The courtyard lies outside, to the north.

On the display case is a violin.

The proprietor watches you quietly from behind the display case.

>show puzzle box to proprietor
The proprietor takes the puzzle box and turns it over in his hands carefully. "Now, this is a tricky one," he says. "They don't make them like this anymore. Frightfully difficult, unless you know the catch of course." His thick, calloused fingers flicker dexterously over the box, sliding a panel here, pressing a corner in there. Suddenly the lid pops open with a faint snick.

He places the box on top of the display case. "And there you have it. A present for you."

>x puzzle box
It's roughly the size and shape of a cigar box, carved from some dark and oddly streaked wood that you can't identify. All six surfaces are decorated in a complex arrangement of grooves and panels, most of which are illustrated with leering, demonic faces and obscene designs. The top is open, folded back on itself to reveal a strange black disk.

>take disk
You take the strange black disk from the puzzle box. It's a dark, glassy circle, about the width of your hand in diameter and half an inch thick in the middle, tapering off to thinness towards the edge like a lens. Its curved surface is cool and perfectly smooth, with the deep, oily blackness of obsidian, though you fancy you can see faint swirls of color inside it.

The proprietor straightens one of the puzzles on a nearby shelf.

>n
The proprietor waves as you leave. "Do come again," he calls.

Hidden Courtyard
This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the southwest leads back into the labyrinth.

>sw

Twisting Lane
The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction.

A symbol spray-painted on a nearby wall marks a side street leading northeast.

>n
You make your way through the dark, tangled streets, navigating a sharp turn that seems to curve inward on itself like a nautilus shell and discovering a series of tiny, abandoned courtyards, their walls slick with mold.

You take several blind turns and suddenly find yourself back . . .

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>w

Narrow Street
As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

>w

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

>s

Whateley Bridge
A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water.

In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind.

>s

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

>e

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>s

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

>w
The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills.

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top.

>se

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open.

>s

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>close door
You close the front door.

>u

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>w

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face.

>pull cord
With a rusty, ratcheting groan and a brief shower of dust, the trap door swings down and unfolds into a rickety wooden ladder that leads up into darkness.

>u
The rungs creak alarmingly as you ascend.

Attic
Dust motes hang motionless in the chill air, and grotesque, misshapen shadows crawl across the slanting rafters. To the east stands a wooden door, half-hidden in the gloom.

A wooden ladder leads down to the second floor.

>e
(first taking the old-fashioned brass key from your pocket)
You unlock the attic door with the old-fashioned brass key.

You open the attic door.

Attic Cell
An austere little cell off the main attic, with a tiny, shuttered window high up on the north wall. A pile of mouldering straw in the corner and what looks like pencil marks on the lower part of the walls suggest that someone lived here once. Or, possibly, was kept here.

>x marks
(the pencil marks)
The marks are a child's drawings, made with pencil or some kind of charcoal. Most of them are smudged and faded, but you can make out stick figures here and there, and the occasional, lollipop-shaped tree.

>x window
The window looks as though it was broken some time in the past, then hastily repaired and nailed shut. Looking closely, you see some faint, round marks scored across the sill.

>x round marks
They look like . . . sucker marks, perhaps? The thought of what could have left marks like that makes you distinctly queasy.

>x straw
The straw is damp and filthy with mold. It's more than a little disquieting to realize that this disgusting mess was someone's bed, once.

>look under straw
You gingerly poke through the filthy straw. Underneath the thickest part of the pile, you discover a tiny gold locket.

>take gold locket
(slipping the old-fashioned brass key into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the gold locket. The oval-shaped locket is held shut with a tiny clasp.

>open gold locket
You open the gold locket, revealing a picture of a young woman inside.

>x picture of woman
The young woman — a girl, really — can't be older than sixteen or seventeen. Her pale, fragile face is framed by straight, dark, mousy hair, and she seems to stare at you with an expression of haunted resignation.

>w

Attic
Dust motes hang motionless in the chill air, and grotesque, misshapen shadows crawl across the slanting rafters. To the east stands a wooden door, half-hidden in the gloom.

A wooden ladder leads down to the second floor.

>d

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling.

>e

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>s

Master Bedroom
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

>drop all
keyring: Dropped.
gold locket: Dropped.
lantern: Dropped.
faculty card: Dropped.

>remove trenchcoat
You take off the trenchcoat.

>drop trenchcoat
Dropped.

>e

Bathroom
A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west.

>bathe
Grateful for the chance to relax, you strip out of your clothes, draw a hot bath (the gas, at least, must be running, even if nothing else in the house is) and soak. The tension of this strange day melts gently from your body. After thirty minutes or so, you begin to feel normal again. After forty-five, you begin to think you might be able to adjust to this place after all. By then the water has begun to grow cool, however, so you drain it and quietly dry yourself off. All in all, you feel enormously better.

>wear clothes
(first taking your clothes)
You put on your clothes.

>w

Master Bedroom
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

You can see your trenchcoat, Michael's faculty card, a lantern, a gold locket, and a keyring with a couple of keys on it here.

>enter bed
You take off your clothes, crawl into bed and pull the covers up to your chin.

>sleep
You lie awake for what seems like hours, listening to the house creak and settle around you and wondering where your husband is. You try to convince yourself that he merely went for a walk and lost track of the time, as he is so often wont to do. In any case, with no telephone and no car, there is little you can do besides hope that he will turn up in the morning. With your worried thoughts chasing themselves endlessly around your mind, you eventually drift off into an uneasy sleep . . .

z




You are running. Down the road, through the woods, where the whippoorwills call their lunatic's song, you are running. The sky boils and thunders overhead. Red-rimmed eyes glare down at you through the branches of the trees. When you look up at them, they merge together, melting into a single, baleful orb — a swollen, bloody moon. Vines and brambles tear at your clothes. Rocks cut your feet. Still you must run, until you suddenly burst out of the underbrush, into a tangled, moonlit clearing.

An ancient, flagstone well stands amidst the weeds in the clearing's center. You are drawn to it. Something is moving inside, scratching at the stones down in that circle of blackness, and somewhere you hear a child's voice whispering: the cellar, Michael is in the cellar, and he is doing something bad . . .

You lean over the well's crumbling lip. Your heart is pounding. Your hair slips from your shoulders, dangling tantalizingly close to the black opening . . .

 . . . the whippoorwills reach a crescendo . . .

Your eyes adjust to the dark and you see —

— you see the most horrible thing —

z


                                       
                                       
                                       
                                       
                                THE THIRD DAY
                                       
                                       
                            If heaven is merciful,
       it will some day efface from my consciousness the sight that I saw,
                   and let me live my last years in peace.
                                       
                              — H. P. Lovecraft,
                              "The Lurking Fear"
                                       
                                       
                                       
z




You wake up, shivering. Michael is in bed next to you, sleeping like a dead man with the sheets in a twisted snarl around his body.

Master Bedroom (on the four-poster bed)
Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east.

Your clothes lie in an untidy pile on the floor.

Michael is sprawled across the bed, fast asleep.

You can also see your trenchcoat, Michael's faculty card, a lantern, a gold locket, and a keyring with a couple of keys on it here.

>get up
Carefully, so as not to wake Michael, you do so.

>wear clothes
(first taking your clothes)
You put on your clothes.

>wear trenchcoat
(first taking the trenchcoat)
You put on the trenchcoat.

>take all
keyring: Taken.
faculty card: You pick up the faculty card. The card identifies your husband as a faculty member at Miskahawnee University, entitled to all the privileges that implies.

lantern: Taken.
gold locket: Taken.

>x michael
Michael is sprawled across the bed with the sheets tangled around his legs, oblivious to the world. Usually, Michael is a light sleeper; you've never seen him so thoroughly knocked out before.

You notice something odd about Michael's feet: they are covered with dried mud.

Michael comes awake with a jerk and a hoarse shout. For a moment or two he only stares about blindly, as if unsure of where he is and all but unaware of your presence. Then, with a grunt of surprise, he seems to come to his senses and painstakingly crawls out of bed.

>ask michael about mud
Michael stops and looks down at his feet. For a moment he looks genuinely dumbfounded. "I . . . I, uh . . . I don't know. I have no idea how that got there." He turns away from you, but not before you see the look of genuine fear on his face. He's not lying, you realize. He really doesn't know.

>talk to michael
"I'm fine . . . I'm fine," grumbles Michael, although he clearly is not. "I'll feel better in a little while."

Michael paces slowly around the room.

>n

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>d

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>n
You open the front door.

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open.

The hairs on the back of your neck prickle as you step outside. Something is wrong. Something is very wrong with the air. A heavy, charged sensation, like standing under high-tension power lines; a fleeting odor of spoiled meat drifting on the wind. You can't put your finger on it, but it might have something to do with that gigantic, swirling hole in the sky.

>x sky
The sky is an unbroken blanket of seething gray clouds in every direction, punctured by a massive, swirling hole.

>s

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>u

Upstairs Landing
A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom.

>w

Upstairs Hall
The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south.

A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling.

>s

Library
Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors opens onto the study, while the hall lies north.

The shelves on the western wall have slid back slightly, revealing a previously hidden safe, currently open.

On the armchair is Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales by Galarian Academius Deschamps.

>w

Study
This room adjoining the library is small and cluttered, though in a comfortable, lived-in sort of way. A writing desk sits under the window, and a large, ornate fireplace fills the northern wall.

The fireplace has been moved to one side, revealing a narrow corridor leading northeast.

Michael's laptop sits in the middle of the writing desk, closed and switched off. Sitting next to it is The Cryptical Haermoniacon.

>ne

Behind the Walls
Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house.

>e
You stumble along as best you can.

Behind the Walls
Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house.

A narrow, wooden ladder leads up.

>u

Observatory
Going by the view through the dingy skylight over your head, you'd guess that you are inside the cupola on the roof of the house. The room is tiny and cluttered, with torn and faded astronomical charts covering every wall. A ladder leads down through a hatch in the floor.

In center of the room stands a large mounted telescope, pointed almost straight up through the skylight.

Framed perfectly in the center of the skylight is an enormous, swirling hole in the sky.

>put disk in slot
(first taking the strange black disk from your pocket)
The black lens fits neatly into the lens chamber and locks into place.

>look in telescope
For a few moments all you can see is a murky, swirling blackness; then patterns of colors begin to emerge, like the rainbow sheen of oil on water. The black disk grows translucent, and stars become visible through the swirling haze. And then . . .

Dear Christ.

Dear Christ, you can see it.

It's . . . it's a comet, roaring silently through the endless void, streaking towards Earth, but it's more than that . . . it's alive, this thing that is coming is ALIVE, with tendrils that must be hundreds, thousands of miles long streaming before it, reaching out to grasp and strangle and devour entire worlds, and it is coming HERE, it is coming to EARTH, and its great, lidless, red-rimmed eye rolls over in an incomprehensibly vast lake of lacrimal fluid, and it

    looks

        at

            you



z




 . . . you wake up some time later, shaking your head. You're not sure what, exactly, you just witnessed: a hallucination, or a genuine astronomical phenomenon, or the physical manifestation of some eldritch god from the another dimension, come to devour the world. You're not even entirely sure you remember what it looked like. The only thing you can clearly recall is a single name, pulsing in your brain like a bruise:

Ialdabaoloth.

