At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open.

A flurry of dead leaves whirls past you in the wind.

>close door
You close the front door.

>nw

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top.

The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone.

>e

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

>s

Forest Lane
The road winds gently through the birch trees south of town, continuing south into the forest. A footpath leads away to the east.

>s

Down the Road
The forest grows denser as you go, crowding up to either side of the road. The groping, tangled branches block out what little sunlight there is left. Beyond the trees, the shadows are impenetrably thick.

The underbrush on the west side of the road is bent and broken, revealing a path into the woods.

>w

Trampled Path
The tangled undergrowth has been beaten down in a path leading roughly from the east to the southwest. Shrubs and grass have been flattened and pushed aside, vines torn down, and small trees bent or even snapped in half, as though something huge and heavy simply trampled its way through.

Something at the edge of the path catches your eye. It's Michael's wallet . . . lying in the mud. In the middle of the woods.

>take wallet
You pick up the wallet. It's just a wallet, an ordinary leather wallet.

The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat.

>sw

Blighted Clearing
The decayed remains of an old building stand here, little more than a shell of crumbling brick surrounded by yellow, sickly grass. A gaping hole that might once have been a doorway provides a way inside, while a path leads northeast.

The forest has become unnaturally quiet all of a sudden. There are no birds calling, no leaves rustling or branches creaking; even the whippoorwills have fallen silent. All is still, holding its collective breath in an expectant hush.

>w

Abandoned Slaughterhouse
The roof has collapsed, leaving the interior open to the sky; the floor is just bare, beaten dirt. Even though nothing stands now but the tottering brickwork, you fancy you can still detect a faint miasma of death — a palpable, chilling reminder of the bloody work that once went on within these walls. Gaps in the bricks lead east and south.

Faint tracks crisscross the dirt inside the ruins.

An old rusty meat hook sticks out of the ground nearby, its point impaling a tattered sheet of drawing paper.

>x tracks
They appear to be tracks left by some bizarre animal — large, quite a bit larger than your outspread hand, and vaguely round like an elephant's footprint. They crisscross the ground in every direction, suggesting that whatever made them either lives here, or visits quite often.

>take sheet
(slipping the wallet into your pocket to make room)
With a slight jerk, you tear the paper free from the impaling meat hook.

>x sheet
It's a pair of crudely rendered figures, scrawled with dark, heavy lines that occasionally punch right through the paper. The two figures are holding hands. The one on the left is a smiling woman with long, straight hair; the one on the right . . . well, it's difficult to say. An octopus with . . . hooves, maybe? Children can be pretty imaginative, but you'd prefer not to speculate on what this child was imagining when he drew this.

Above the first figure is scribbled the word "MOMY"; above the second, "WILAM (ME)".

>take hook
(slipping the tattered drawing into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the meat hook. The crossbar fits in your palm, leaving the hook part to stick out between your third and fourth fingers. It's a heavy thing, nearly fifteen inches long from handle to point, made for hauling around carcasses with a minimum of ceremony. You wouldn't like to think what this could do to a living person.

>s

Tangled Thicket
A dense, tangled thicket grows right up to the wall here, impenetrable in every direction. Rising from the midst of the underbrush is the top of an ancient well.

>x well
A squat circle of mortared stone, about as high as your waist. Its mouth is covered with a lid of rotting plywood.

>pull plywood
You dig your fingers under the edge of the plywood and, straining as hard as you can, slide the heavy cover off the top of the well and onto the grass.

>d
Carefully testing each foothold, you descend into the well.

Bottom of the Well
The stone walls press in on you from all sides, and the sky is just a dim circle of light, far above you.

You are standing knee deep in a rattling jumble of children's bones.

>x bones
You can deduce only two things about the gruesome pile: there are more bones here than could be produced by a single child, and some of the bones are older than others. That means that whoever has been throwing them down here has been doing it for a long time.

>u
With palpable relief, you climb up out of the well.

Tangled Thicket
A dense, tangled thicket grows right up to the wall here, impenetrable in every direction. Rising from the midst of the underbrush is the top of an ancient well.

The plywood cover leans against the side of the well.

>n

Abandoned Slaughterhouse
The roof has collapsed, leaving the interior open to the sky; the floor is just bare, beaten dirt. Even though nothing stands now but the tottering brickwork, you fancy you can still detect a faint miasma of death — a palpable, chilling reminder of the bloody work that once went on within these walls. Gaps in the bricks lead east and south.

Faint tracks crisscross the dirt inside the ruins.

>e
You are about to step back through the eastern wall when a noise makes you stop. In the woods, outside — something is there. Something breathing. Something huge.

A branch cracks sharply, and then another. It's coming this way.

>s

Tangled Thicket
A dense, tangled thicket grows right up to the wall here, impenetrable in every direction. Rising from the midst of the underbrush is the top of an ancient well.

The plywood cover leans against the side of the well.

The sound of tearing undergrowth grows louder. Whatever it is, it's practically bulldozing its way through the forest.

>d
As quickly as you are able, you scramble down the rough stone walls. Ten feet from the bottom, you slip, landing with a clatter amidst the dusty bones.

Bottom of the Well
The stone walls press in on you from all sides, and the sky is just a dim circle of light, far above you.

You are standing knee deep in a rattling jumble of children's bones.

The ground trembles with the thing's footsteps as it thunders up the path. It must be right outside the slaughterhouse!

>hide under bones
You burrow down into the bones, piling more bones on top of you for cover while trying to make as little noise as possible.

You hear it pause inside the building, its hide slithering loudly against the brick walls as it moves about. There is a horrible, wet snorting sound — as if it is trying to pick up a scent — then an unspeakable, inhuman grunt of anger.

>wait
Time passes.

Scarcely daring to breathe, you lie as still as possible beneath your cover of bones as the thing, whatever it is, moves closer. A shadow falls across the mouth of the well, and then the circle of light above you is blotted out by a shape so utterly hideous that it is all you can do to remain conscious. Two grotesquely blue and childlike eyes blink down into the darkness of the well, searching . . .

wait



And then it is gone.

You must have lost consciousness after all, judging from the stiffness in your limbs and the difference in the light. You have no idea how long you were out, but the creature must have passed you by. The forest is still again; the breathing and heavy footsteps, gone.

Involuntarily, you let out a long, shuddering breath.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>stand
The bones fall away, clattering loudly as you stand up.

All the digging around has uncovered a child's teddy bear amidst the bones.

>take bear
(slipping the meat hook into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the teddy bear. It's an old and well-loved bear, fur worn through to the stuffing in several places. Stitched onto its behind are the initials, J.G.

>x bear
It's an old and well-loved bear, fur worn through to the stuffing in several places. Stitched onto its behind are the initials, J.G.

>u
With palpable relief, you climb up out of the well.

Tangled Thicket
A dense, tangled thicket grows right up to the wall here, impenetrable in every direction. Rising from the midst of the underbrush is the top of an ancient well.

The plywood cover leans against the side of the well.

>n

Abandoned Slaughterhouse
The roof has collapsed, leaving the interior open to the sky; the floor is just bare, beaten dirt. Even though nothing stands now but the tottering brickwork, you fancy you can still detect a faint miasma of death — a palpable, chilling reminder of the bloody work that once went on within these walls. Gaps in the bricks lead east and south.

Faint tracks crisscross the dirt inside the ruins.

>e

Blighted Clearing
The decayed remains of an old building stand here, little more than a shell of crumbling brick surrounded by yellow, sickly grass. A gaping hole that might once have been a doorway provides a way inside, while a path leads northeast.

>ne

Trampled Path
The tangled undergrowth has been beaten down in a path leading roughly from the east to the southwest. Shrubs and grass have been flattened and pushed aside, vines torn down, and small trees bent or even snapped in half, as though something huge and heavy simply trampled its way through.

>e

Down the Road
The forest grows denser as you go, crowding up to either side of the road. The groping, tangled branches block out what little sunlight there is left. Beyond the trees, the shadows are impenetrably thick.

The underbrush on the west side of the road is bent and broken, revealing a path into the woods.

The deranged cry of a whippoorwill echoes through the trees.

>n

Forest Lane
The road winds gently through the birch trees south of town, continuing south into the forest. A footpath leads away to the east.

A small group of townsfolk has gathered up at the north end of the road. As you approach, still breathless from your encounter with the thing at the slaughterhouse, they turn towards you . . . and that's when you notice that the one in the lead has an axe in his hand. And the one behind him has a pair of garden shears.

Two of the men move over to your left, and several more step out of the forest behind you. All of them carry weapons or nasty farm implements of some kind, and all of them look as though they're just about ready to put an end to a certain over-curious, outsider woman who doesn't know enough to keep her nose out of things better left undisturbed.

A flurry of dead leaves skitters along the ground past you in the wind.

In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind.

>e
You dart past the men and sprint down the footpath. The townsfolk do not follow you, apparently confident that there is no way you can escape.

Churchyard
An old, abandoned church sits here in an overgrown clearing, surrounded by a sagging wrought-iron fence. A gap in the fence leads back west, while a bare path beaten through the weeds leads around the church to the southeast.

>se
The path curves to the northeast as it follows the side of the church.

Behind the Church
Back here the forest grows right up to the fence, parts of which are literally buried in the tangled underbrush. All of the windows on this side of the church are boarded up as well. A path leads southwest, around to the front of the church.

A wooden trap door over by the corner of the building probably leads into a cellar.

>open trap door
The trap door is held shut with a rusty padlock.

>hit padlock with hook
(first taking the meat hook from your pocket)
You raise the meat hook high over your head and bring it squarely down onto the padlock. The rusted metal casing splits open, the hasp falls loose, and the padlock, now just a broken shell, slides to the ground.

>open trap door
You open the trap door.

>enter trap door
As you drop to the floor, the trap door suddenly slams shut above you. Something thumps against it from the outside. You hear low voices: someone muttering something you can't quite catch, and a brief round of unpleasant laughter. Then the voices fade away.

Furnace Room
Centipedes and beetles scurry across the rotten floorboards as you shine your light across the room. The hulking mass of black metal over in the corner is probably an old coal-burning furnace. A dark alcove leads north, and a trap door in the ceiling leads back up to the outside.

A foul stench fills the air. At the very edge of your light's glow, you can just make out a horribly suggestive shape lying in the shadows behind the furnace.

>x shape
It's the dead body of a woman. Her head has been raggedly hacked away, and her clothes above the waist are soaked through with blood. As you fight to control your rising gorge, you note that the body cannot have been here for very long; the decay, though bad, is not very far advanced. She must have been killed within the last couple of days at most.

>search body
Trying to avoid the worst of the blood, you lift the woman's lapel, hoping to find something — identification, maybe. Her wallet is missing; however, you do find, tucked into the pocket of her blazer, a small, steel key.

>take steel key
(slipping the meat hook into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the small steel key. It looks like the kind of key that would open a desk drawer.

>x furnace
It's a big, pot-bellied iron furnace, blackened from decades of use. Fat, twisting ducts sprout from its back and snake their way up to the ceiling. A small hatch on one side is closed.

A twitch of movement from the corpse makes you jump. When you look again, you see it's only an insect crawling across its blouse.

>open hatch
Your throat tightens as you open the bloodstained hatch . . .

Slow horror washes over you. You found exactly what you expected to find, of course. It's the real estate agent's severed head.

>n

Empty Stairwell
This alcove used to house a staircase leading up and down; now it contains only a few rotted boards jutting from the walls and an empty shaft dropping down out of sight.

Above you hang the broken-off remains of the stairs leading up to the ground floor, the last shattered riser only a couple of tantalizing feet out of reach.

>turn on flashlight
The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam.

>turn off lantern
You cup your hand over the top of the glass and blow out the flame.

>put all in trenchcoat
small steel key: You slip the small steel key into your pocket.
lantern: You slip the lantern into your pocket.
faculty card: You slip the faculty card into your pocket.
keyring: You slip the keyring into your pocket.

>jump
You take a few steps back, draw in a deep breath, and make a dash for the edge. At the last possible second you jump, sailing out over the pit as you make a desperate grab for the bottom riser — and catch it.

The riser bends ominously under your weight but doesn't give. Panting, you haul yourself up onto the creaking stairs.

Vestry (on the broken stairs)
A dimly lit side-chamber, long stripped of any furniture or decoration. A creaky wooden staircase leads down to the basement, and the main chapel lies to the south.

Piled in one dusty corner is a heap of old cloth.

The creaking under your feet is getting louder.

>get off stairs
You get off the broken stairs.

Vestry
A dimly lit side-chamber, long stripped of any furniture or decoration. A creaky wooden staircase leads down to the basement, and the main chapel lies to the south.

Piled in one dusty corner is a heap of old cloth.

>take cloth
You shake the cloth out, revealing it to be a hooded robe.

From the stairwell behind you there is a sudden, painful shriek of nails ripping out of stone; a loud, protracted splintering; and finally a thunderous crash that echoes up the shaft from far below.

>x robe
The robe is made of some rough, heavy material, dyed a deep, murky red, like old wine. Its voluminous folds would easily cover you head to toe.

>s

Chapel
A leaning pulpit looks out over rows of broken and overturned pews. Shards of shattered stained glass grit under your feet. The main entrance of the church, to the west, is solidly boarded up, but there are open doorways to north and south.

The big wooden cross that once hung above the pulpit now lies on the floor amidst the rest of the debris.

On the pulpit is a massive black book.

>x cross
The cross is built of thick wooden beams, split and cracked down its nearly ten-foot length but still sturdy. From the angle at which it fell, it looks as though it originally was hung upside down.

>x pulpit
Where once fiery-tongued preachers harangued congregations of quaking Puritans, now there is simply a dusty wooden box on its end. Sitting atop it is a huge, black-bound tome.

>x tome
It lies open atop the pulpit, thicker than an unabridged dictionary. Its thousands of yellowed, crinkly pages are bound in a strange black material that looks like some kind of hide but clearly isn't leather. Your first thought was that it might be a Bible, but a glance at the pages is sufficient to tell you otherwise. The text within is arranged in two columns, the first in what looks like Latin, the second a translation in English. There are illustrations, as well, horrible icons that make your skin crawl to look at.

>read tome
As you lean closer over the pages to make out the words in this dim light, the letters seem to writhe and crawl across the page, twisting themselves into strange combinations, horrible words that you've never seen before . . . and yet, somehow, you understand their repulsive meaning. Something about a "Blessed Event" that will occur very soon, and a hideous god whose name may not be spoken . . .

Is this the book that was preached in this church before it fell to ruin? Are these the gods these people worshipped? You want to tear your eyes from the page in revulsion, but some small, gleefully filthy part of you wants to keep reading, to uncover the secrets that the citizens of Anchorhead have always known . . .

Will you read on?

>no
You tear yourself away from the podium with a shudder.

>s

Ringing Chamber
This narrow, high-ceilinged room sits underneath what remains of the church steeple. To the north a doorway leads back to the chapel.

A length of rope dangles down through a hatch in the ceiling.

>climb rope
You haul yourself up onto the rope, bringing your whole weight to bear. It holds for about three seconds — then there is a sharp snap from above you and a muffled clank. The rope falls, spilling you to the floor amidst an untidy tangle of coils.

>take rope
You pick up the length of rope. It's about twenty feet long, frayed in a few places but still sturdy enough.

>n

Chapel
A leaning pulpit looks out over rows of broken and overturned pews. Shards of shattered stained glass grit under your feet. The main entrance of the church, to the west, is solidly boarded up, but there are open doorways to north and south.

The big wooden cross that once hung above the pulpit now lies on the floor amidst the rest of the debris.

On the pulpit is a massive black book.

>push cross north
With a bit of shoving and grunting, you manage to wrestle the heavy cross into the vestry. At least, partly — the crossbeam won't quite fit through the doorway.

Vestry
A dimly lit side-chamber, long stripped of any furniture or decoration. The stairs leading down have collapsed, leaving an empty shaft that drops away into darkness. The main chapel lies to the south.

The big wooden cross is partially wedged in the doorway.

>tie rope to cross
You loop the length of rope around the wooden cross and knot it tight.

>put robe in shaft
The hooded robe drops out of sight, and a few moments later you hear a faint, distant splash.

>d
Gripping the end of the rope tightly in both hands, you lower yourself off the edge and shimmy down into the darkness.

Dangling in Darkness
You are at the end of the rope, feet dangling over empty space. Far below, you can barely make out the faint glimmer of your light reflecting on water.

>let go of rope
You take a deep breath, shut your eyes tight, say a quick prayer, and let go of the rope.

The heart-stopping plunge lasts barely a second before you hit shockingly cold water. Everything goes black. Water rushes into your mouth and nose; a hard stone surface slams into you and nearly knocks the wind out of you; you twist and claw for air . . . and then suddenly the ground is beneath your feet, and your head breaks the surface. Blindly, you thrash your way to dry ground and fall to your hands and knees, choking and sputtering, feeling cold brick beneath your fingers.

Vault
This half-flooded chamber's crumbling brickwork must have been built much earlier than the rest of the tunnels. The ceiling is lost in darkness above you, but judging from the echoes it must be very high. Water trickling in from the tunnel to the northwest has pooled at the room's far end, which is submerged nearly waist-deep.

You can see a hooded robe here.

>take robe
Taken.

>nw

Sewer Tunnel
The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north.

A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight.

>u
You climb out through the opening.

Under the Bridge
Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level.

An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, open to the darkness below.

>se
You take the stairs back up to the street.

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>s

Chilly Avenue
It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town.

>w
The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills.

Scenic View
Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top.

A gust of wind blows your hair into your face.

>se

Front of the House
Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house.

At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion.

>s
You open the front door.

Foyer
A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open.

Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer.

>s

Back Hall
A short hallway, connecting rooms to the east, west and north.

>e

Kitchen
The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house.

>e

Pantry
The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back stands open, revealing dark stairs leading down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west.

>d

Cellar
Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor.

A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall.

>w

Wine Cellar
This wing of the cellar looks like it was part of an earlier structure, older than the rest of the house, with rough walls made of unmortared stone. Tall, heavy wine racks lean in on all sides — all of them empty. An exit to the east leads back to the main cellar.

One of the racks has been pulled aside, revealing an intricate mural on the north wall.

You can also see a broom here.

>take broom
You pick up the broom.

>x mural
The entire north wall is covered with a bas-relief mural depicting the same pattern of stars you saw on the astronomical charts up in the observatory. Now that you know what you're looking for, you can see that the arcs and lines clearly divide the stars into distinct constellations, each corresponding to one of the grotesque zodiac symbols from the charts: the Priest, the Toad, the Formless Mother, the King in Tatters, the Piper, the Black Herald, the Crawling Flame, the Many-Angled Gate, the Gnawer of Graves, the Spiraling Worm, the Sleeper Under the Waters, and the Mask.

>touch gate
You place your fingertips on the raised bumps of the constellation representing the Many-Angled Gate and press. There is a brief vibration, and a crackling as though the air were charged with electricity. The stars beneath your fingers begin to glow with a green, eldritch light.

>touch gate
You place your fingertips on the raised bumps of the constellation representing the Many-Angled Gate and press. The stars pulse briefly, then resume their steady glow.

>touch toad
You place your fingertips on the raised bumps of the constellation representing the Toad and press. The stars begin to glow.

>touch black herald
You place your fingertips on the raised bumps of the constellation representing the Black Herald and press. The stars begin to glow.

>touch mask
You place your fingertips on the raised bumps of the constellation representing the Mask and press. The stars begin to glow.

Suddenly, all four constellations begin pulsing steadily, glowing brighter with each pulse. The hairs on the backs of your arms stand up as the charge in the air grows in intensity. There is a sharp crack. A hot, foul-smelling wind gusts out at you from around the edges of the mural. And then the entire wall slides to one side with a deep grinding noise, revealing a dark passageway leading north.

>n

Great Stair
The walls of this passageway are carved from solid rock with unearthly precision: ten feet wide, ten feet high, perfectly smooth and perfectly square. Behind you, an opening in the wall leads back to the wine cellar. To the north, the stone floor drops away in a series of wide steps carved into the living rock.

>n
You start down the massive steps . . .

Halfway
The stair seems endless, vanishing into the shadows above you, plunging down into the black, unplumbed depths beneath the hill.

>n

Temple Entrance
At the base of the stairs stands a massive door of greenish metal, barring the way north.

The door is enormous, nearly fifteen feet on a side, and made entirely of some greenish metal that reflects your light with an oily, wavery sheen. There is no handle, no keyhole or latch of any kind — nothing but the smooth metal with a single symbol etched into its center.

>x door
The door is enormous, nearly fifteen feet on a side, and made entirely of some greenish metal that reflects your light with an oily, wavery sheen. There is no handle, no keyhole or latch of any kind — nothing but the smooth metal with a single symbol etched into its center.

>say ialdabaoloth
The blasphemous syllables crawl loathsomely from your lips. Just hearing the sound of it makes you nauseous. Nature draws back from the presence of it like skin peeling away from the edges of an infected ulcer.

The metallic door rings out as if struck. It vibrates in aching harmony with the lingering echoes of the Name, a shrill, grating sound like a dentist's drill. The air grows thick, and terrible shapes waver and melt in the air before you — shapes that twitch and move with hungry purpose, that seem to look at you as they float by — and then the ringing fades away. There is a moment of stillness, and then the gleaming door swings smoothly open on perfectly balanced and utterly silent hinges.

>n
You pause at the threshold while your eyes adjust to the strangely phosphorescent gloom. It is nearly a full minute before you can make out enough details to see what this place is, and almost a full minute more before your mind can accept what you are seeing . . .

Burial Mound
A pale, watery glow sifts down from somewhere above, illuminating a cavern transformed into a warehouse of the dead. Hundreds of mummified human corpses lie on stone shelves carved into the walls. An altar of black stone, flanked on either side by what appear to be a pair of metal pillars, sits at the north end of the gigantic cavern.

Commanding your attention over everything else, however, is an enormous monolith rising up from the cavern's center, all the way up to the dome-shaped ceiling. Its color and texture are subtly different from the surrounding rock, and you are puzzled at first by a nagging certainty that you have seen something like it before . . . until at last you realize that what you first took to be nothing more than a large supporting pillar is, in fact, the true base of the town square obelisk.

>x monolith
It is simply enormous, nearly ten feet to a side and rising straight up through the ceiling, penetrating what must be a hundred feet of intervening rock before breaking the surface far, far above. Carvings, deep and untouched by eroding weather, cover every inch of the obelisk's surface.

>x carvings
There are carvings on the north, east, south, and west sides of the obelisk. They seem to tell a story as you walk slowly around the base, though it's not exactly clear where the story begins and where it ends.

>x north carvings
The carvings here look to be, if anything, even older than the ones on the surface; they look less like an alien alphabet and more like primitive picture-writing. The northern pictogram depicts a circle of stick-men, dancing or worshipping around a shape like an upturned bowl (perhaps a nearby hill, or perhaps this very mound you're standing in was once above ground). One figure stands on top of the mound, raising something — a stone, possibly, although it's shaped more like a teardrop — above a second stick man who is bent backwards over an altar. Wavy lines radiate from the altar down to the base of the mound. Some kind of energy or radiance, or streams of blood, maybe?

>x east carvings
The eastern pictogram depicts a fiery starburst, decorated with trailing lines (perhaps indicating a comet or shooting star?) descending from the sky, raining fire and death upon the stick-men. Two shining objects — more teardrop stones — fall from the starburst, and are carried away by the stick-men.

>x south carvings
The southern pictogram depicts the stick-men, again dancing in a circle around the mound.

>x west carvings
The western pictogram depicts the stick-men constructing a tower of stones near the mound, for some unclear purpose. Hanging overhead is the comet, drawn in such a way as to suggest a watchful eye; it appears to be looking down upon the tower with approval. Wavy lines radiate from the eye in all directions, and several of them converge to join at the tower's tip.

>x pillars
Each column is approximately seven feet high and perfectly cylindrical, made of some oily, greenish metal. They vibrate very slightly, almost imperceptibly; only a thin shimmering around the edges and the faint, atonal ringing noise betray any motion at all.

>play flute
(first taking the strange metal flute from your pocket)
The strange harmony of the flute blends with the atonal ringing coming from each of the two columns, and the three sounds suddenly grow stronger, resonating and reinforcing one another, intertwining like a dissonant, invisible braid. It grows louder and louder until your eardrums ache and your vision blurs. Suddenly the air above the altar begins to ripple, as though with extreme heat. Space seems to twist and buckle between the two columns — then, with a sound like a wet sheet being torn slowly down the middle, it splits.

You are immediately swept off your feet by a howling wind that pulls everything within reach towards the portal. Dust and debris; bones and loose rock; everything not nailed down goes flying across the temple and into the all-devouring maw hovering over the altar-stone. Desperately, you wedge your fingers into a crack at the base of the monolith and hang on for dear life as the wind tries to claw you away. You scream, and even the sound of your voice is whipped away, pulled over your shoulder like a trailing ribbon and sucked into whatever blasphemous dimension lies beyond that horrible rift. The ground is shaking, loosening your grip, and for a heart-stopping moment you realize that you aren't going to be able to hold on . . . and then the chaos stops, leaving you breathless on the floor.

Painstakingly, you pry your stiff, bleeding fingers out of the crack and roll over. The rift is gone. The ground is still. The air is normal, and the columns are ringing quietly, as if nothing at all had happened.

>s

Temple Entrance
At the base of the stairs stands a massive door of greenish metal, open and beckoning you towards the shadow-filled vastness to the north.

All of the shaking has caused part of the wall to split, revealing a jagged passage leading northeast.

>ne
You squeeze your way through the rough, narrow tunnel.

Sewer Tunnel
The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north.

A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight.

Part of the wall on the other side of the channel has collapsed, revealing a jagged passage leading southwest.

>n
The tunnel narrows and descends sharply as you go.

Flooded Passage
Knee-deep water floods this cramped tunnel, dripping constantly from the low ceiling and seeping through thousands of invisible fissures in the walls. There is barely enough room to stand up straight, much less stand somewhere dry. Higher ground lies to the south, while a metal grating blocks the way north.

Some of the concrete surrounding the grating has shifted and partially collapsed, causing a couple of the bars to bend outward. You might be able to just squeeze through.

>n
It's a tight fit, but with the help of a lot of profanity and the unpleasantly slippery sludge that coats everything down here, you manage to push yourself through the narrowly spaced bars.

Storm Tunnel
Water pours into this tunnel from dozens of small drain pipes jutting from the walls. Most of the sewage flows south, but a thin trickle is diverted into a larger concrete pipe that leads away to the east.

>enter pipe
(the concrete pipe)
After crawling on your hands and knees for some distance, you reach the end of the pipe and wriggle your way out, dropping awkwardly to the sand.

Littered Beach
A narrow strip of sand tucked away between two outcroppings in the rocky shoreline, accessible only by way of a steep, muddy slope to the west. The sand is filthy and strewn with rocks, seaweed, and other bits of storm-tossed detritus.

Near the bottom of the slope, a sewage outflow pipe juts out over the beach, about three feet above the ground. A thin stream of acrid-smelling sewage trickles out over the lip of the pipe, forming a puddle in the sand.

>w
You pick your way up the slope, push the loose board aside, and slip back in through the gap.

Garbage-Choked Alley
This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence.

A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder dangles just a few feet above the cobblestones.

>nw

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>e

Office
Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and the glass-paneled door to the west is open.

Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine.

Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold.

>x desk
(the paper-strewn desk)
On the paper-strewn desk are a telephone, an answering machine, some scattered papers, and a styrofoam cup full of some cold, murky coffee. The desk also has one small drawer, which is closed.

You hear a fly buzzing around right around your head.

>unlock drawer
(first taking the small steel key from your pocket)
You unlock the drawer with the small steel key.

>w

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

>drop steel key
Dropped.

>e

Office
Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and the glass-paneled door to the west is open.

Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine.

Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold.

>open drawer
You open the drawer, revealing a hastily written letter and a tarnished bronze key.

>take hastily written letter
You take the hastily written letter from the drawer. It consists of a page torn from a yellow legal pad, covered on both sides, top to bottom, with erratic, frightened handwriting. It is dated two days ago — the day you arrived in Anchorhead.

>read hastily written letter
The letter reads:

My name is Claudia Benson. If you are reading this anywhere within the city limits of Anchorhead then you need to know that your life is in GRAVE and IMMEDIATE danger. Most of the people in this town are members of a dangerous cult. They are fanatical and insane, and will KILL anyone who learns their secrets. I received a phone call early this morning which I believe is a threat to my life. I assume the cult somehow discovered that I was planning to divulge information to an outsider and now intends to silence me. From what I've been able to discover, the cult worships some sort of demon or god from outer space, which they believe visited the earth millions of years ago and will return some day soon to rule the planet, or maybe destroy it — I am unclear on the exact details as there is very little information to be found, unless the University is hiding material from me which it may well be. They believe that their god will return very soon — in fact it is coming THE DAY AFTER TOMORROW. They are building something in the old paper mill, some sort of device to attract their god's attention and bring him to this world. They are almost finished. In TWO DAYS they will turn on the device and perform a ritual that they call the "Blessed Event". I have no idea whether this device actually will actually do what they say it does, but I have every reason to believe that this ritual will mean the WHOLESALE SLAUGHTER of every man, woman and child in the area who is not a cult member.

If you are the young man who planned to move into the Verlac estate, you must be careful. The cult is watching your every move. They will attempt to induct and brainwash you or, failing that, MURDER both you and your wife. I had hoped to stay and warn you, but as you can see I don't have much time left. With any luck, I'll be gone by tonight. If I don't make it, please try to stop these people. This key might help — I found it in our old property file on the lighthouse. I know they consider that building important for some reason. Do what you can with it. Be careful. These cultists are a menace, every one of them inbred and insane, and no one is safe as long as they are allowed to run free.

Good luck,

 — Claudia Benson

>take bronze key
(slipping the hastily written letter into your pocket to make room)
You take the tarnished bronze key from the drawer. The old, heavy key is crusted over with a thin rime of verdigris.

>w

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

You can see a small steel key here.

>w

Narrow Street
As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

>w

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

To the south, a group of townsfolk has gathered on Whateley Bridge.

>n

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat.

>w

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

>n

Shanty Town
The road from the south peters out at a small clearing of rutted mud, crowded close on all sides by leaning, dilapidated shacks.

The town is still, almost deserted. Only a few loose bits of tarpaper, flapping listlessly in the wind, betray any movement.

>read newspaper
(the Innswich Herald)
The front page story is about Jeffrey Greer, 8 years old, who was abducted from his home at #38 Mill Town Road last night. Little Jeffrey is the latest victim in a series of abductions that stretches back for years, and that authorities believe is the work of a single perpetrator. Early investigations led some to suspect Edward Verlac, Anchorhead resident and professor of history at Miskahawnee University, but local police were unable to obtain a confession or any hard proof. Verlac had been convicted of murdering his wife and two daughters in January of this year; he was found not guilty by reason of insanity and incarcerated in Dalmers Asylum, where he remained until committing suicide last March. This latest kidnapping, occurring months after Edward's death, seems to have cleared up any lingering suspicions that he might have been the culprit. Anyone possessing information regarding the whereabouts of Jeffrey Greer is strongly urged to speak to the authorities as soon as possible.

There's also a weather advisory down in the lower corner of the page: heavy offshore fog today and tomorrow. Boaters and fishermen are urged to stay on shore; if you must go out on the water, navigate by the buoys and make sure to set a beacon before casting off.

>knock on shack 38
After a moment or two of silence, you hear a furtive rattling, and the door opens a crack to reveal a woman's pale and haggard face. She stares out at you with a mixture of trepidation and mistrust.

>x woman
(the woman)
She is pale and thin from lack of food. Her hair is prematurely gray. The dismal poverty and constant fear that she has lived in all her life have leached her body of its youth, leaving her aged before her time, stooped and scarred with worry. She clutches a tattered shawl, its fabric the faded color of old dishwater, close around her throat, and she watches you the way a wounded rabbit might watch a potentially hungry cat.

>give bear to woman
(the woman)
(first taking the teddy bear from your pocket)
The woman's eyes widen with sudden recognition, and she takes the teddy bear from you, turning it over to read the initials stitched on the back. "Where did you find this?" she whispers. "Did you find —" she stops, looks around at the other hovels. "Come inside," she says, and motions you into the house.

#38 Mill Town Road
A dirty, ramshackle home, scraped together out of the bits and ends of working-class poverty. The floor is warped and broken wood, and rags hang across the window in lieu of curtains. Over in the far corner is a dented stove; in the opposite corner a threadbare cot. It hits you suddenly that a family of three once lived, slept and ate all in this single room, which is slightly smaller than your bedroom back at the house.

An old wooden oar is propped up in the corner.

With gentle deliberateness, the woman places the battered teddy bear on the cot, propping it up against the pillow and arranging the cover around it as though it were an ailing child. "Where did you find this?" she asks, not even daring to turn around. "Did you find Jeffrey? Is Jeffrey all right?"

>tell woman about jeffrey
Quietly, and sparing as much detail as you can, you tell Jeffrey's mother about the old well behind the slaughterhouse, and what you found at the bottom of it. When you are finished, she stands still for a moment; then her knees buckle and she sits down suddenly on the edge of the cot. She looks as though she might throw up. "Thank you," she says finally in a trembling voice. "That was . . . difficult to hear. But it's better to know. Thank you for telling me. If there is anything I can do, or give, to help you, please just ask."

>ask woman about mill
"They work us like animals," she says bitterly. "They pay us barely enough to live, and then we give it right back to them to pay for rent, food, and goods from the company commissary. And when someone complains, or snoops around too much, they disappear, and the company calls it an 'accident'."

>ask woman about oar
"Oh, that belonged to Max. He used to take the boat out sometimes, when the weather was good. It's pointless hanging on to it, I suppose. I don't even know where the other one got off to. But it's really the only thing I have left to remember him by."

>ask woman about max
"Max was my husband," she says, a sad, wistful smile on her face. "He worked at the mill . . . until the 'accident'. Her smile turns bitter. "That was almost a year ago. I miss him so much . . . He took care of Jeffrey and me."

>take oar
You pick up the oar.

"Go ahead, take it," the woman says. "I'm so grateful to you for telling me about Jeffrey . . . even if it wasn't what I'd hoped to hear. If it can help you in any way, I want you to have it."

The woman frets uncomfortably.

>s
"Oh — you're leaving?" the woman asks. "Come back soon."

Shanty Town
The road from the south peters out at a small clearing of rutted mud, crowded close on all sides by leaning, dilapidated shacks.

In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind.

