As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

>e

Outside the Real Estate Office
A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast.

You can see a small steel key here.

>se

Garbage-Choked Alley
This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence.

A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder dangles just a few feet above the cobblestones.

>e
Dropping to your hands and knees, you wriggle underneath the loose board and scramble down the muddy slope.

Littered Beach
A narrow strip of sand tucked away between two outcroppings in the rocky shoreline, accessible only by way of a steep, muddy slope to the west. The sand is filthy and strewn with rocks, seaweed, and other bits of storm-tossed detritus.

Near the bottom of the slope, a sewage outflow pipe juts out over the beach, about three feet above the ground. A thin stream of acrid-smelling sewage trickles out over the lip of the pipe, forming a puddle in the sand.

>enter pipe
(the outflow pipe)
Well, you've already been through this pipe from the other end, and at this point you can't get much dirtier than you already are, so . . . taking a deep breath, you duck your head and clamber into the pipe.

Storm Tunnel
Water pours into this tunnel from dozens of small drain pipes jutting from the walls. Most of the sewage flows south, but a thin trickle is diverted into a larger concrete pipe that leads away to the east.

>s

Flooded Passage
Knee-deep water floods this cramped tunnel, dripping constantly from the low ceiling and seeping through thousands of invisible fissures in the walls. There is barely enough room to stand up straight, much less stand somewhere dry. Higher ground lies to the south, while a metal grating blocks the way north.

Some of the concrete surrounding the grating has shifted and partially collapsed, causing a couple of the bars to bend outward.

>s

Sewer Tunnel
The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north.

A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight.

Part of the wall on the other side of the channel has collapsed, revealing a jagged passage leading southwest.

>drop oar
Dropped.

>light match
(first taking the book of matches from your pocket)
One of the matches flares up and starts to burn.

>light lantern with match
(first taking the lantern from your pocket)
The wick catches, and soon the lantern is flickering brightly.

>put matches in trenchcoat
Not while it's lit!

The match burns down to your fingers and goes out.

>take oar
(slipping the book of matches into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the oar.

>u
You climb out through the opening.

Under the Bridge
Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level.

An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, open to the darkness below.

>se
You take the stairs back up to the street.

Riverwalk
This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east.

>e

Vacant Lot
Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west.

A filthy old mattress lies among the weeds over in one corner of the lot.

You can also see a flask here.

>e

Wharf
A ramshackle wooden pier extends several yards out into the ocean. Several fishing boats are tied nearby. The sea is calmer today, quiet but for the slow creak of hawsers and hollow slap of water against hull. To the west, a path leads back to land.

At the end of the pier stands a lone wooden post, like a half-finished gibbet.

Tied to the end of the pier, somewhat apart from the larger boats, is a small dinghy.

You hear the lonely clanging of a buoy, somewhere far out to sea.

>x post
(the wooden post)
The post is about six feet high, with a big iron hook sticking out near the top.

>put lantern on hook
(the iron hook)
You hang the lantern from the iron hook.

>enter dinghy
You lower yourself carefully into the tiny, pitching boat.

>x dinghy
It's small and weather-beaten, but it looks seaworthy. There's a pair of oarlocks attached to either side; only one oar, though. The dinghy is tied to the pier by a short mooring line.

Another wave crashes against the rocks, sending a cloud of spray into the air.

>put oar in oarlock
You slide the oar through the metal ring and lock it into place.

>untie dinghy
You slip the knots holding the dinghy to the pier. Immediately the little boat drifts out to sea with you in it.

The current carries you further out to sea.

Out to Sea (in the dinghy)
You are floating far from shore, clinging to the sides of the dinghy as it rides the sickening swells. An impenetrable fog hangs over the water, obscuring vision in every direction except to the west, where you can just make out the faint glimmer of the lantern, lighting your way back to shore.

>listen to buoy
It sounds like the buoy is somewhere off to the northeast.

>ne
You row for a while through the fog.

Out to Sea (in the dinghy)
You are floating far from shore, clinging to the sides of the dinghy as it rides the sickening swells, surrounded by fog and sea. To the west, you can just make out the faint glimmer of the lantern, lighting your way back to shore.

>listen to buoy
It sounds like the buoy is somewhere off to the east.

>e
You row for a while through the fog, towards a blurry shadow in the distance. As you draw closer, the shadow resolves itself into the shape of an old warning buoy.

Out to Sea (in the dinghy)
You are floating far from shore, clinging to the sides of the dinghy as it rides the sickening swells, surrounded by fog and sea. To the west, you can just make out the faint glimmer of the lantern, lighting your way back to shore.

An old, rusted buoy rocks gently on the swells nearby. Chained to its side is a human skeleton.

>listen to buoy
The buoy groans and clanks softly as it sways back and forth.

The bell clangs softly as the buoy is rocked by a passing wave.

>tie dinghy to buoy
You tie the dinghy's mooring line to part of the buoy's scaffolding.

>x skeleton
It slumps against the base of the buoy, legs dangling in the water. Birds and fish have long ago stripped the corpse down to sea-bleached bones, but it still wears the tattered remnants of a workman's coveralls.

>x coveralls
The coveralls hang from the bones in ragged, salt-stiff strips. Stitched over the breast pocket, you can barely make out the name "Max".

>search skeleton
In the pocket of the coveralls is a long steel key.

>take steel key
You take the long steel key from the coveralls. It's an oddly shaped key, a long cylinder with several rows of peg-like teeth set radially around it.

The wind moans fretfully, like someone mourning something lost.

>x buoy
Six feet of rusty scaffold, mounted on a hemispherical base and topped with a heavy iron bell. The sides of the buoy are streaked with brownish-yellow stains, left by what must have been many deliquescing corpses over the years.

>untie dinghy
You untie the dinghy's mooring line from the buoy.

The bell clangs softly as the buoy is rocked by a passing wave.

>w
You row for a while through the fog.

Out to Sea (in the dinghy)
You are floating far from shore, clinging to the sides of the dinghy as it rides the sickening swells, surrounded by fog and sea. To the west, you can just make out the faint glimmer of the lantern, lighting your way back to shore.

>w
You row for a while through the fog.

Out to Sea (in the dinghy)
You are floating far from shore, clinging to the sides of the dinghy as it rides the sickening swells, surrounded by fog and sea. To the west, you can just make out the faint glimmer of the lantern, lighting your way back to shore.

>w
You spot land, and row towards it.

Wharf (in the dinghy)
A ramshackle wooden pier extends several yards out into the ocean. Several fishing boats are tied nearby. The sea is calmer today, quiet but for the slow creak of hawsers and hollow slap of water against hull. To the west, a path leads back to land.

At the end of the pier stands a lone wooden post, like a half-finished gibbet. A lantern dangles from a hook sticking out of its side.

>tie dinghy to pier
You moor the dinghy securely to the pier.

>get out
You get out of the dinghy.

