As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole.

The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone.

>w

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

To the south, a group of townsfolk has gathered on Whateley Bridge.

>n

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

>w

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

>sw

Old Foundation
The foundations of an older structure lie crumbling in a sunken square of ground, here in the mill's shadow. Weeds push through cracks in the buckled concrete. Rusted pipes and bent rebar poke up from the ground like the broken legs of dead insects. Dense thickets surround the ruins on all sides, although narrow, overgrown trails lead west and northeast through the underbrush.

Absently, you brush a bit of ash from your clothes; it leaves a grimy smear.

>search thickets
Pushing the prickly branches aside, you discover a metal hatch set into the base of the wall.

>unlock hatch
You unlock the maintenance hatch with the long steel key.

>open hatch
You open the maintenance hatch.

>n
After a short crawl through the steam-filled duct, you emerge onto a factory floor.

Factory Floor
Towering banks of machinery rise up on all sides, assaulting you with waves of heat and noise. The air is thick with steam. To the north, a narrow aisle squeezes between the churning, vibrating machines, while a maintenance tunnel to the south leads back outside.

Mounted to one side of the machines is a large metal valve wheel, beneath a glowing red warning light.

>put towel on wheel
(first taking the bath towel from your pocket)
You drape the bath towel over the wheel.

>turn wheel
You grip the sides of the wheel. Even through the fabric of the bath towel, you can feel the heat radiating from it. The wheel squeaks reluctantly at first, but you haul on it with all of your strength and suddenly it gives all at once. From somewhere overhead there is a massive, rushing hiss, and the warning light goes dark. The machines are still rattling and clanking away, but they are no longer constantly emitting blasts of scalding hot vapor.

>remove trenchcoat
You take off the trenchcoat.

>drop trenchcoat
Dropped.

>drop all
lantern: Dropped.
broom: Dropped.
hooded robe: Dropped.

>take broom
You pick up the broom.

>take fuse
You take the old fuse from the pocket of your trenchcoat.

>take flashlight
You take the flashlight from the pocket of your trenchcoat.

>n
Turning sideways, you inch your way into the aisle separating banks of frantically running machinery.

Control Station
The machines give way to a relatively open area, making room for several banks of control panels. To the north stands an old-fashioned cage elevator, while a narrow aisle leads back through the machinery to the south.

>x panels
The control panels are covered end to end with blinking lights, unlabeled knobs and buttons, and gauges with needles holding still, creeping slowly up the dial, or swinging wildly back and forth. It is entirely unclear what any of the controls do or whether you might not just blow the whole paper mill sky-high if you messed with them. On the other hand, the large red button labeled "elevator" seems relatively straightforward.

>enter elevator
You slide both the inner and outer gates open.

You get into the elevator.

The "walls" of the elevator are composed of latticed metal bars, with diamond-shaped holes wide enough to fit your arm through. There is an inner gate and an outer gate, designed to open and close together, both of which are folded back.

Bolted to the inside of the elevator is a small control box.

>close gates
You slide the rattling gates closed.

>push red button with broom
(poking the broom through the elevator's latticed metal bars)
You tap the red button with the end of the broom. The cage lurches suddenly and begins its rattling descent. The inner and outer gates slide past each other like a pair of giant cheese graters, instantly splintering the broomstick into several pieces that clatter to the bottom of the shaft. A few moments later, the elevator shudders to a halt.

Workshop (in the elevator)
Outside the elevator, benches cluttered with scraps and bulky machining tools fill the space in this dim workroom.

Bolted to the inside of the elevator is a small control box.

>get out
You slide both the inner and outer gates open.

You get out of the elevator.

Workshop
Benches cluttered with scraps and bulky machining tools fill the space in this dim workroom. To the north stands an old-fashioned cage elevator.

A large blueprint is spread out across one of the workbenches.

You notice a leather tool pouch lying amidst the clutter.

>x blueprint
It's a technical schematic for some extraordinarily complicated device. It somewhat resembles a massive lamp or beacon, surrounded by a complex orrery of lenses, mirrors, flanges, coils, and other, more arcane components, all of it connected by an impenetrable tangle of circuitry. Its function is impossible to determine, although several prominent arrows point to a small compartment on one side, which seems meant to house some sort of crystal-shaped power source.

Roughly scribbled underneath this are the words, "Obed — make sure to use PHILLIPS head for the focusing chamber!"

>take blueprint
Taken.

>take pouch
You pick up the tool pouch. It's a scuffed leather bag designed to be worn around your waist, big enough to hold a few small tools.

Inside the pouch is a screwdriver.

>enter elevator
You get into the elevator.

Bolted to the inside of the elevator is a small control box.

>x control box
It's a small metal housing with a white button on one side, and a small panel that looks like it can be unscrewed.

>unscrew panel
(first taking the screwdriver from the tool pouch)
You carefully loosen the screws and open the panel. The tiny compartment contains a few wires and an empty socket, meant to hold a fuse.

>put fuse in socket
(first taking the old fuse from the tool pouch)
You plug the old fuse firmly into the socket.

>close gates
You slide the rattling gates closed.

>push white button
The cage lurches suddenly and begins its rattling ascent. A few moments later, the elevator shudders to a halt.

Control Station (in the elevator)
Outside the elevator, the machines give way to a relatively open area, making room for several banks of control panels. A narrow aisle leads back through the machinery to the south.

Bolted to the inside of the elevator is a small control box.

>get out
You slide both the inner and outer gates open.

You get out of the elevator.

Control Station
The machines give way to a relatively open area, making room for several banks of control panels. To the north stands an old-fashioned cage elevator, while a narrow aisle leads back through the machinery to the south.

>s
Once again you edge through the aisle.

Factory Floor
Towering banks of machinery rise up on all sides, assaulting you with waves of heat and noise. The air is thick with steam. To the north, a narrow aisle squeezes between the churning, vibrating machines, while a maintenance tunnel to the south leads back outside.

Mounted to one side of the machines is a large metal valve wheel with a bath towel draped over it.

You can also see a hooded robe, a lantern, and your trenchcoat here.

>wear trenchcoat
(first taking the trenchcoat)
You put on the trenchcoat.

>take all
hooded robe: Taken.
lantern: (slipping the tool pouch into your pocket to make room)
Taken.
bath towel: (slipping the blueprint into your pocket to make room)
You pluck the bath towel from the wheel.

>s
You crawl back through the duct and emerge into the cool, outside air.

Old Foundation
The foundations of an older structure lie crumbling in a sunken square of ground, here in the mill's shadow. Weeds push through cracks in the buckled concrete. Rusted pipes and bent rebar poke up from the ground like the broken legs of dead insects. Dense thickets surround the ruins on all sides, although narrow, overgrown trails lead west and northeast through the underbrush.

Set into the base of the mill wall, nearly covered by the thickets, is an open metal hatch.

You hear a faint scuffling noise behind you, and then something slams into the back of your head with enough force to set off sparks in your vision. The ground tilts and slides underneath you, and you drop to your hands and knees. As you struggle to not pass out, your assailant walks slowly around to stand in front of you. Terror and nausea wash over you in alternating waves. You are too weak to look up and see his face, but you recognize his shoes easily enough. After all, you helped him pick them out not three weeks ago.

More footsteps. You sense a number of men standing around you. "We'll take care of her, Your Holiness," rasps an old, weathered voice. Rough hands start to drag you to your feet.

"No," says Michael, and the hands pause.

"Your Holiness, she has seen too much," protests the raspy voice. "She must be killed."

"That won't be necessary," Michael says. "Put her somewhere out of the way, where she can't get into any more trouble. I'll deal with her myself . . . after the Blessed Event."

The horror and pain are too much; the darkness overwhelms you. You try to catch a glimpse of your husband's face as the men drag you away, but in your clouding vision all you can see are a pair of burning, red-rimmed eyes . . .

z


                                       
                                       
                                       
                                       
                                THE LAST NIGHT
                                       
                                       
                   We live on a placid island of ignorance
                   in the midst of black seas of infinity,
               and it was not meant that we should voyage far.
                                       
                              — H. P. Lovecraft,
                            "The Call of Cthulhu"
                                       
                                       
                                       
z




Numb.

You float slowly to consciousness like a bubble rising to the surface of some warm, thick oil. You are numb.

For several minutes, that is as much as your battered mind can handle, that one thought: you are numb.

Then your head becomes a bit clearer, and you connect the thought with the sensations trying to make their way through to your foggy brain. You are numb. Your arms. You can't feel your arms at all.

You try to get up but your limbs won't wok; you flop over onto your stomach, struggling like a worm beached on a drying sidewalk. You roll onto your back and manage, by a process of spasmodically crunching and kicking your legs, to achieve a sitting position. At last, your vision clears and you get a good look around, and that is when the horror of your situation finally slams home . . .

Padded Cell
You're in a cell, barely eight feet by eight feet square, with walls and floor covered in thick, quilted padding. Even the cell door in the north wall is padded, except for a tiny observation window through which feeble light trickles in.

>kick door
You slam your shoulder into the door. It rattles violently and you stagger back. Your shoulder is throbbing, but it looks like the door is hanging a bit loose in its frame . . . maybe?

>kick door
With a defiant shout, you launch yourself at the door with all of your strength. Something loudly snaps (door? or shoulder? you wonder, half-coherently), and the door flies open, sending you sprawling out into the hallway.

Hallway
Grim hallways of concrete and institutional tile lead north, east, and west. A cell door hangs half off its hinges to the south.

>e

Hallway
Grim hallways of concrete and institutional tile lead north and west. A cell door lies south, its observation window marred with an old bloodstain.

A barred window with a cracked frame looks out to the east. Lying on the floor beneath it is a shard of glass.

A sizzling flash of lightning from outside burns away the shadows, etching everything around you in stark black and white.

>take shard
Painstakingly, you get down on your knees and very, very carefully pick up the shard of glass with your teeth.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! ***

A monstrous explosion of thunder crashes outside, shaking the floor beneath your feet.

>x window
(the barred window)
The window has seen some damage in the past. The glass is partially broken, the frame is covered with clusters of strange, round marks, and a network of cracks — some of them quite large — mars the surrounding wall.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>put shard in cracks
Tilting your head, you manage to wedge the shard of glass into one of the wider cracks.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening peal of thunder crashes outside.

>cut straitjacket with shard
Carefully, carefully, you lean back against the wall and start shrugging your shoulders against the jutting glass shard. After what seems like an eternity, you feel the straps begin to fray and loosen, until suddenly the jacket grows slack and slips free. You shake the horrible garment off in disgust, giving it a vicious kick for good measure as you rub the feeling back into your arms.

You hear muted tittering coming from somewhere in the halls.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>x cell door
It's a sturdy-looking wooden door, with a small observation window allowing you to peek into the cell.

An ugly brown smear of dried blood obscures the inner surface of the observation window. Several strands of dark hair are still stuck to the congealed stain.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another boom of thunder crashes outside.

>unlock door
You slide the bolt back, unlocking the door.

>open door
You open the cell door.

>s

Padded Cell
The padding in this room seems a bit worse for the wear. Murky bloodstains spatter the canvas, and there is a large tear down in one corner, with bits of stuffing leaking out. The door to the north is open.

>x tear
The canvas is frayed and jaggedly torn, as though someone gnawed through it with their teeth. Stuffed down into the space behind the tear is a torn square of canvas.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>take canvas
(the torn square of canvas)
As you pull the piece of canvas from its hiding place, something pricks you sharply on the finger. Peering into the tear once again, you notice an old, crusty needle embedded in the stuffing.

You hear barely audible whispering echoing through the hallways outside.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening clap of thunder crashes outside.

>take needle
You take the needle from the ragged tear. The point is worn down but still sharp. It's crusted with what looks like old, dried blood.

>read canvas
(the torn square of canvas)
It's roughly six inches to a side, covered front and back with tiny, barely legible handwriting. The letters are the faded brown color of dried blood.

My name is Edward Verlac, and this is my testament:

I am utterly, utterly mad.

I am imprisoned in this padded cell, my arms bound in a straitjacket, for my own safety and for the safety of others. It is no less than I deserve. I write this testament using a needle dropped carelessly by one of the orderlies some weeks ago; biting my lip to draw blood for the ink, I hold the tiny steel quill between my teeth to write. This painstaking and exhausting task shall be my last on earth. When I am finished, I shall dash my brains out against the observation window of my cell door. The last of the Verlacs will be dead, and the world made a slightly brighter place thereby.

Know this: I did not slip into madness through any weakness or congenital defect, except insofar as my blood is indelibly tainted with the cursed ichor of the Verlac line. It is the shadow of my own history, and my foolish insistence on apprehending it in full that has destroyed my sanity — the piecing together of dissociated knowledge that has opened up such terrifying vistas of reality, and of my frightful position therein.

From as early as I can remember, I have been aware of a presence. Throughout my childhood it watched me, stalking the boundaries of my dreams and solitary thoughts, like a predator sniffing the perimeter of its prey's enclosure. My mother laughed and praised my zealous imagination when I told her of these feelings, but each night she bade me never to remove the strange amulet that she had placed around my neck while I was still in the crib. When I asked her why, she did not respond. Now I understand all too well.

Mother died while I was still young, and the memory of her urgent but cryptic warnings faded soon after her death. I became embarrassed by the vague and unvoiced aura of scandal that seemed to hover over my family name like a pall, and mortified by the superstitious trinket that I still wore around my neck, as if in ignorant servitude to some forgotten myth. I had not seen the red-rimmed eyes in my nightmares since my twelfth birthday, and I dismissed them as immature bugbears that had passed along with my boyhood. At the age of twenty-three, I pawned the amulet at a shop in town, and thus sealed my doom.

It was an amateur's interest in genealogy, and a guileless curiosity regarding my own origins, that led me many years later to discover that I am in fact the progeny of my own grandfather, Mordecai Verlac, and his tormented daughter — my mother, Anna. My shock upon learning this can scarcely be described. For many months afterwards I was in a daze, adrift within a tempest of rage, shame, and self-loathing. This was nothing, however, compared to the horrors that my further investigations would soon uncover. For my grandfather's sins were but the latest iteration in a grotesque chain of incest stretching all the way back to the first of my ancestors to set foot on this continent's soil. Furthermore, these were not merely the indiscretions of a degenerate and interbred clan, but part of a deliberate ritual that has been performed again and again down the centuries to preserve and sustain through multiple generations a single animating spirit. Mordecai was merely the fleshly guise for an ancient and pernicious warlock — my grandfather ten generations removed — Croseus Verlac.

For more than twenty years I had lived free from his foul presence, growing ever more complacent and unguarded, but the day I discovered the truth of my heritage, all that changed. As though the knowledge itself were the final hammer-blow smashing through the rotting mortar of invisible bulwarks I did not even realize protected me, the daemonic encroachment of Croseus rushed in to overwhelm me. I had no trinkets to aid me now; I had only the strength of my will, and it was not enough. I found my mind flooded at the oddest times with strange, antiquarian memories; my own speech and mannerisms acquiring uncharacteristic, archaic patterns. I suffered lapses in memory, and black moods of rage and contempt for which I could discern neither object nor motive. Worst of all, I found myself looking at my dear daughter Gertrude — God, only nine years old! — in the most unwholesome way, with the most unmentionable ideas creeping through my subconscious.

I have scoured every street in this cursed town looking for that shop, but I have never found it. I have delved into esoteric histories and forbidden grimoires, searching for some solution to my plight, but I have discovered no answers, only further horrors. For Croseus does nothing without purpose, and his centuries of schemes and bodysnatching are all bent towards a single endgame. He plans to unleash a primordial evil such that the earth could not possibly survive. I have seen the plans and the blueprints. I have read the dread tome in my great-great-great-great grandfather Elijah's church. I have used the black lens that is neither glass nor any mineral known in this world, and I have looked upon what that fiend means to call down! He and this town, his foul congregation of fanatics and madmen! They are all a part of it! They have been watching me, waiting for me to change, waiting for him to emerge, waiting for the return of their monstrous high priest!

It was then that I realized that killing only myself would not be sufficient, for the inhabitants of this town would hunt down my family and wreak a terrible vengeance if I took from them their Messiah. And besides, who could say whether the devil might not concoct some new means of entering the world, through a new body — through that of one of my daughters, perhaps? The ritual has traditionally demanded a grandson, but Croseus was never one to let such a trivial inconvenience stop him. He would find a way. They are still his blood. He always returns to his blood.

So I did the only thing I could do. Julia, Sonia, Gertrude, I am so terribly sorry. I could not let him have you, and I will not let him have me. The denizens of Anchorhead grow impatient for my transformation. Last night something smashed the window outside my cell, and I heard its thick, hideous appendages prying at the bars. I must delay no longer.

The reinforced glass, against my skull — if I can find the strength — there will be no blood for him once I am gone.

good-bye —

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

