You hear another bang. At the far western end of the hall, you see a cell door rattle in its frame. Looks like someone else is trying out your brilliant escape plan.

>wear trenchcoat
You put on the trenchcoat.

With a final bang, the door flies open. A hairy, ragged man staggers out into the hall. He looks around and whispers something to himself in a thin, giggling voice. You notice with no small amount of disquiet that he is not wearing a straitjacket. Then he notices you, and grins.

And that is the moment when the lights go out.

It is now pitch dark in here!

>w
You feel your way nervously through the dark.

Darkness
It is pitch dark, and you can't see a thing.

You hear someone thumping towards you in the dark, very close by.

>w
You feel your way nervously through the dark, trying to stay clear of the madman's heavy breathing.

Darkness
It is pitch dark, except for a dimly flickering illumination coming from a stairway leading down.

>d
You make your way downstairs by the flickering fluorescent lights.

Hallway
This short hallway ends in a set of stairs leading up. A barred gate blocks the way east.

A man wearing an orderly's uniform lies face down in the next room, several feet beyond the gate.

Fetched up against the bars is a magazine.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>pull orderly's key with umbrella
(first taking the umbrella from your pocket)
Carefully, you snag the key with the hook-shaped handle of your umbrella and give it a yank. It snaps free from the orderly's belt and slides close enough for you to reach through and grab it.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening explosion of thunder crashes outside.

>take orderly's key
(slipping the umbrella into your pocket to make room)
You pick up the orderly's key. It's a long, flat key with a loop on one end

>unlock gate with orderly's key
You unlock the barred gate with the orderly's key.

>open gate
You open the barred gate.

The madman thumps into view at the top of the stairs, breathing heavily and whispering.

>e

Waiting Room
A grubby little waiting room, decorated in mint-green tile and white-washed cinderblocks. A potted palm clings tenaciously to life over in one corner. You can exit the asylum to the east, and the barred gate to the west stands wide open.

The orderly lies face down in the middle of the room.

You can also see a magazine here.

The madman limps into view off to the west.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>e
The rain is coming down, so you open your umbrella.
(first taking the umbrella from your pocket)

Asylum Courtyard
This tiny, shadowed court is dominated by the grim edifice of Dalmers Asylum to the west. A driveway to the northeast leads back to the street, and a narrow alley leads to the east.

The madman comes shuffling out the front door of the asylum and suddenly stops dead in his tracks. He looks around, dumbfounded. He looks up at the sky, and blinks as raindrops strike his skin. A grin slowly spreads across his face as it finally dawns on his addled brain that he is outside the asylum — that he is, at long last, free. With a wild whoop of pure joy, the madman hobbles away down the street, giggling and chattering to himself and ignoring you completely. For a moment, you almost feel happy for this dangerously psychotic cannibal that you have just let loose into the world at large.

And then he is gone, one more shadow amidst the town's shadow-haunted streets.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening boom of thunder rolls across the night sky, shaking the whole town to its foundations.

>turn on flashlight
The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam.

>e

Hidden Alley
A featureless alley hidden deep in the shadows behind the courthouse. The opening to the northeast affords an excellent view of the whole town square and the obelisk at its center.

>ne

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

As you step into the open square you hear a sudden commotion — several crowds of people are converging on the town square from different directions.

>sw

Hidden Alley
A featureless alley hidden deep in the shadows behind the courthouse. The opening to the northeast affords an excellent view of the whole town square and the obelisk at its center.

The obelisk glows red with the bloody light of their torches. They are almost here!

>remove trenchcoat
You take off the trenchcoat.

A gust of wind blows your hair into your face.

Dozens of robed figures pour into the town square, brandishing torches and rallying around the obelisk in the center. You huddle back into the shadows of the alley, but no one even looks in your direction.

>drop trenchcoat
Dropped.

Several of the cultists appear to be struggling with someone. They drag the captive forward to a clear space in front of the obelisk and force him to his knees. His wrists are bound, and a rough sack covers his head. One of the cultists raises his hands to the mob, then snatches the sack away — revealing the terrified face of the old man from the vacant lot.

>wear robe
You slip the robe on and pull the hood up to cover your face.

The rain slackens off momentarily to a weak drizzle, then returns afresh in a brief, freezing downpour.

"Brethren!" shouts the leader, his voice hoarse from the effort of being heard over the crowd. "We are gathered here to mete out justice upon one who has sinned against us. This one," — he jabs a finger at the old man — "has divulged our secrets to an outsider and betrayed the trust of our brotherhood! For this, his body shall be broken, and his soul condemned forever to the necrotic folds of the Womb of Nehilim!"

The crowd lets loose with a roar of approval, while the old man throws back his head and shrieks helplessly into the storm.

>ne

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk.

The square is packed with an angry mob of cultists, screaming hungrily for the old man's death.

The wind moans around eaves and over rooftops, like someone keening over a grave.

Suddenly the old man wrenches himself free and bolts through the crowd. He gets about twenty feet before ending up, by some grotesque twist of luck, right in front of you. He stops short. His eyes widen in recognition. He opens his mouth to speak . . . and that's when half a dozen cultists tackle him and pull him down.

As the old man's body hits the ground, a small, shiny object falls out of his clothes and bounces out across the cobblestones, unnoticed by all.

>x old man
The old man twists helplessly in his captors' grip, rolling his eyes in near-animalistic terror.

Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning.

The cultists carry the old man back to the obelisk and lift him up, hanging him by the wrists from the iron ring set into the obelisk's tip.

"Please," sobs the old man, "please, I didn't, I swear, I didn't tell nobody, so I didn't! I SWEAR TO GOD I DIDN'T TELL —"

His pleas abruptly turn to incoherent screams as the cultists crowd around the base of the obelisk and begin beating his legs and torso with long, metal rods. The old man's screams stop when his ribs are staved in; after that you have to avert your eyes. Soon all you hear are the horrible, meaty thuds, and the wet snapping sound of breaking bones.

>take amulet
Surreptitiously, you pick the amulet up from the cobblestones and slip it into your sleeve.

It seems to go on forever, although in truth it probably lasts for less than a minute. Eventually the crowd backs away, their cheering and chanting diminished to a low, hypnotic murmur. The cultists quickly disperse back into the streets. Soon the square is once again empty . . . except for the mutilated thing hanging from the obelisk.

>wear amulet
You put on the amulet.

Another flash of lightning crackles across the roiling clouds.

>x obelisk
The obelisk measures about three feet square at its base, narrowing slightly as it rises a good fifteen feet to a blunt tip. It seems to be a monument of some sort, although you can see no plaque or marker anywhere near it. Dense, twisting hieroglyphs cover all four sides, partially obscured by rain-washed streaks of blood.

Embedded in the stone high up on one side, very near the top, is an iron ring, from which the old man's body hangs.

You shrink back into the shadows of a nearby doorway as a group of cultists passes by, heading north. They continue on their way without looking in your direction, and you breathe a quiet sigh of relief. Even though the robe effectively conceals you, you can't be too careful.

A flurry of dead leaves swirls past you in the wind.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening peal of thunder rumbles across the heavens.

