Hidden Alley
A featureless alley hidden deep in the shadows behind the courthouse. The opening to the northeast affords an excellent view of the whole town square and the obelisk at its center.

You can see your trenchcoat here.

The rain beats down ceaselessly.

>take trenchcoat
Taken.

The rain slackens off momentarily to a weak drizzle, then returns afresh in a brief, freezing downpour.

>ne

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. The old man's mutilated body hangs from its tip like a shattered marionette.

>n

Whateley Bridge
A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torrential water.

As you approach the midpoint of the bridge, you see a hulking shadow lurking on the far bank. You pause warily, remembering the townsfolk who threatened you earlier, but as the shadow moves closer, you realize that it can't possibly be a person. It's much too large, and the shape is . . . wrong. Through the obscuring rain you can make out a tottering, barrel-like trunk, a crown of writhing branches, or something more like vines . . .

A sudden crack of lightning illuminates the bridge for a single, paralyzing instant. Your mind recoils. Already the details are mercifully fading, but you cannot, and will never, scour from your memory the one image picked out in sharp chiaroscuro by the lightning flash: the thing's awful, misplaced face . . . its squalling mouth . . . its grotesquely blue and childlike eyes . . . the face of the boy in the silver locket, that hideous monster into which William Verlac has grown.

The monster bawls like an angry infant crying for its mother.

>give gold locket to monster
(first taking the gold locket from the trenchcoat)
(first closing the umbrella)
You toss the gold locket at the monster's feet. It lands face up on the cobblestones, revealing the young woman's face framed inside. The monster bends over awkwardly, grasps the chain with the tip of one of its tentacles, and lifts the locket up to dangle in front of its face.

For a single heartbeat of time, even the storm seems to hold still. The monster gazes at the picture in the locket for a long, aching moment, and a glob of thick, greasy liquid wells up in the corner of its eye and oozes down its malformed cheek.

The monster ignores you for the moment, completely fascinated by the locket.

>hit monster with hook
(first taking the meat hook from the trenchcoat)
With a hoarse yell, you raise the meat hook over your head and fly at the monster while its attention is distracted. It turns just as you bring the weapon down, and by sheer luck the point sinks deep into one of its huge, blue eyes. It screams horribly, a high-pitched, child's scream, and rears back, knocking you to the ground. One of its tentacles manages to pluck out the meat hook, and a thick, black ichor spurts from the wound, splattering you as you scramble to get out of the monster's way.

The monster staggers over to the side of the bridge, moaning and weeping blood. It crashes into the stone parapet, overbalances, and goes over the side. In another flash of lightning you see it hit the water with a giant splash and disappear, engulfed by the raging current.

A sudden gust blows a cold spray of rain into your face.

>put all in trenchcoat
bath towel: You slip the bath towel into the pocket of your trenchcoat.
torn square of canvas: You slip the torn square of canvas into the pocket of your trenchcoat.

>drop trenchcoat
Dropped.

>take bronze key
You take the tarnished bronze key from the pocket of your trenchcoat.

>take screwdriver
You take the screwdriver from the tool pouch. It's an ordinary Phillips-head screwdriver.

>take needle
You take the needle from the pocket of your trenchcoat.

>take crystal
You take the asymmetrical crystal from the pocket of your trenchcoat.

