North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening explosion of thunder blasts across the sky.

>n

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

A group of cultists passes you, heading east. They nod and murmur greetings as they pass, thinking you one of them. Looking out over the heath, you see dozens more by the distant lights of their torches, traveling in groups of two or three or half a dozen, all making their way towards the lighthouse.

The rain slackens off momentarily to a weak drizzle, then returns afresh in a brief, freezing downpour.

>w

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

>sw

Old Foundation
The foundations of an older structure lie crumbling in a sunken square of ground, here in the mill's shadow. Weeds push through cracks in the buckled concrete. Rusted pipes and bent rebar poke up from the ground like the broken legs of dead insects. Dense thickets surround the ruins on all sides, although narrow, overgrown trails lead west and northeast through the underbrush.

Set into the base of the mill wall, nearly covered by the thickets, is a metal hatch.

>w

Railroad Tracks
The trail from the east climbs up a short embankment and emerges from the thicket here, alongside the railroad tracks. Beyond them lie miles of empty heath as far as you can see.

The train lumbers into view, its rumbling growing louder as it approaches.

>put crystal on tracks
You put the asymmetrical crystal on the railroad tracks.

The ground begins to vibrate as the rumbling grows into a roar. The whistle lets out an urgent shriek. The train is bearing down quickly now, very, very close.

>wait
Time passes.

The clouds overhead mutter restlessly to themselves.

With a sudden, hot gust of wind and a tremendous blast of noise, the train hurtles past you. Boxes and flats flicker by like frames in a kinetoscope. The ground is literally shaking, sending bits of gravel bouncing down the sides of the embankment.

The train rolls right over the asymmetrical crystal. There is a sudden spark of light as the crystal goes under the wheels, and a painfully loud ringing sound. Something shiny thumps into the grass at your feet.

Another flash of lightning burns across the roiling clouds.

>wait
Time passes.

As abruptly as it arrived, the train is past. The last swaying car recedes quickly down the line to the southwest.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another crack of thunder shakes the night sky.

>wait
Time passes.

With a last, mournful groan of its whistle, the train disappears from sight.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

>wait
Time passes.

A sudden gust blows a cold spray of rain into your face.

>l
Railroad Tracks
The trail from the east climbs up a short embankment and emerges from the thicket here, alongside the railroad tracks. Beyond them lie miles of empty heath as far as you can see.

You can see a flawed asymmetrical crystal here.

>take flawed crystal
Taken.

>e

Old Foundation
The foundations of an older structure lie crumbling in a sunken square of ground, here in the mill's shadow. Weeds push through cracks in the buckled concrete. Rusted pipes and bent rebar poke up from the ground like the broken legs of dead insects. Dense thickets surround the ruins on all sides, although narrow, overgrown trails lead west and northeast through the underbrush.

Set into the base of the mill wall, nearly covered by the thickets, is a metal hatch.

>ne

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

>e

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

Another group of cultists passes you, heading east.

The clouds overhead mutter restlessly to themselves.

>e
The road reaches the shore and heads out onto a rocky breakwater.

The Spine
You are picking your way across the rough, rock-strewn spine of the breakwater, where the road has diminished to little more than a pair of muddy ruts. A hundred yards to the northeast, at the breakwater's far end, a lonely lighthouse stands vigil against the ocean.

>ne

Foot of the Lighthouse
You stand in a circular clearing among the stones, surrounded on nearly every side by the sea. Before you looms the lighthouse, a massive pillar of ancient, pale bricks, jutting defiantly at the sky. The road leads southwest from here, and it looks as though a path leads around the outside of the lighthouse to the east.

Set into the thick, stone wall of the lighthouse is a bronze door.

Another group of cultists passes you, heading east, around the side of the lighthouse.

>unlock door
The key turns reluctantly, and the door unlocks with a muffled clunk.

>ne
With great effort, you manage to pull the heavy door open a few inches, enough to slip through.

Choosing a moment when no cultists are walking by, you slip in through the open door.

Service Room
The inner workings of the lighthouse, all of the equipment and machinery, have been cleared out long ago, leaving only an empty room with bare, cinderblock walls. Concrete steps spiral two hundred feet up into hazy shadows.

The bronze door to the southwest is ajar, throwing a wedge of dim light into the lighthouse interior.

Another flash of lightning burns away the shadows.

>u
You climb the winding stairs, circling the inner circumference of the tower and finally emerging, breathless, at the top.

Lantern Room
Walls of floor-to-ceiling glass surround this circular chamber at the top of the lighthouse, allowing a spectacular view in every direction. Stairs lead back down to ground level.

An extraordinarily complicated device stands in the middle of the room.

   *** ka-WHAM!!! *** — Another deafening peal of thunder crashes outside.

>x device
The device somewhat resembles a massive lamp or beacon, surrounded by a complex orrery of lenses, mirrors, flanges, coils, and other, more arcane components, all of it connected by an impenetrable tangle of circuitry. Some of its pieces don't even look like metal. They're more like . . . body parts, twitching tissues and curving insect shells, all somehow bolted and welded in with everything else.

The glow seems to be coming from a small, covered compartment built into the base of the device.

>x compartment
The small compartment is screwed shut, but you can see a purplish-white glow shining out around the edges

>unscrew compartment
You carefully loosen the screws and open the compartment. In the compartment is a flawless asymmetrical crystal.

>drop screwdriver
Dropped.

>take flawless crystal
As you pull the crystal loose, the purple glow fades.

"Well, well, if it isn't the loyal wife."

You whirl around to see Michael standing at the top of the stairs, flanked by a pair of burly cultists.

>x michael
Michael is gone. Something inside him, some sickness or fever, has burned away everything you once recognized in your husband. You can see it, still in there: something gleeful and wicked and very, very old, staring out at the world through sunken, red-rimmed eyes.

"You are a persistent little tart, I'll give you that," Michael says as he advances on you. "Quite the gutsy little irritant. I suppose Michael must have been one of those modern men, didn't go in for the submissive type." He sneers. "In my day, we knew how to keep our wives. A headstrong woman should be broken, just like a headstrong horse."

>give flawed crystal to michael
"Thank you," Michael says acidly, snatching it from your hand.

Michael walks over to the strange device and runs his fingers along its twisted contours. "At least you haven't managed to do too much damage . . ." He peers into the small compartment in the device's base. "Though you certainly tried."

>wait
Time passes.

He holds the crystal up to the light. "Tears of the Nameless Watcher, they are called. Said to be formed from droplets of vitreous humour cast off when the God last passed near this planet, long millennia ago. There have always been two of them, perfectly identical in every way. So you see, your feeble attempts at sabotage could never have worked." He snaps the crystal into the compartment, where it begins to glow with a flickering, purplish radiance. "We always had a spare."

As if on cue, the guards seize you by the arms and begin dragging you downstairs.

"You have worked so hard, and sacrificed so much," Michael says, following behind you, "trying to understand who my family was, what we have been trying to accomplish here. I think it is time to show you. I think you'll find it quite enthralling."

They haul you down to the rocks behind the lighthouse, across the gangplank to the island that has appeared just beyond the breakwater's tip, and into the midst of the crowd of chanting cultists. They tear away your robe, and the crowd howls with bloodlust. The guards then force you to kneel in the squelchy muck and manhandle you into a pair of handcuffs bolted to a heavy stone block. From there you must crane your neck to watch the ceremony taking place, and your husband, or whatever thing your husband has become, leading the terrible rites.

Island of Flesh
The surface of the island is covered with an ankle-deep layer of reeking slime dredged up from the ocean's floor. Beneath the muck, the ground throbs, heaving rhythmically to a blasphemous, living pulse.

The deranged people of Anchorhead press close on every side, chanting and swaying to the hypnotic pulse that rises up from the depths of the fleshy ground. Two of them stand to either side of you, standing guard.

Michael stands in the middle of it all, basking in the bloodlust of the crowd.

A few feet away is a young boy, tied to a tall wooden stake.

>wait
Time passes.

Michael takes a torch from one of the crowd. Holding it high above his head, he turns to the lighthouse and intones:

"Iach! Watcher Who Is Named Not, I call upon Thee! Formless Drifter of the Gulfs Between, I summon Thee!"

The crowd murmurs in ecstatic encouragement.

>x boy
This must be Jeffrey Greer, the boy who was kidnapped from his home three days ago. His face is pale and pinched with malnourishment, and he stares about with eyes glazed with terror.

"The stars are right! The House of the Thirteenth Sign is in ascendance!"

Michael traces a sigil in the air with the flaming torch. The flames seem to hang for a moment in strange patterns before twisting away into nothing.

"I cast aside the Seals! I throw open the Gates!"

The torch flares up with a roar, and the ocean waves respond with sudden inexplicable fury, crashing brutally against the island.

>x sky
The clouds overhead are a boiling expanse of blackness shot through with flickering bursts of sheet lightning. The huge, spiraling hole over the lighthouse seems to be the center of it all, gradually drawing all of the storm's fury into itself as it turns.

Michael turns his back to the lighthouse, facing out to sea.

"To the East, where the Spawning Chaos seethes and suppurates within the Crucible of Grum, from whose bursting pustules arise the Million Unseeable Forms, I summon Thee!"

>wait
Time passes.

Michael makes a quarter-turn to his left.

"To the North, where the Howling Hunger sweeps invisibly across the Yellow Plains of Pnakoth and gnaws upon the entrails of the pious, I summon Thee!"

A monstrous bolt of lightning licks down from the sky and strikes the sea just north of the island, sending a spume of steam and boiling water fifty feet into the shrieking sky. Screams of fear and rapture erupt throughout the pressing crowd, almost inaudible beneath the deafening peal of thunder.

>wait
Time passes.

Michael isn't even fazed. He makes a half-turn to the right and continues:

"To the South, where the Seventeen Corpulent Sultans of S'laas-tha stand in judgment over the Apostates of the Vermiform Heresy, and force their vile copulations upon the repentant, I summon Thee!"

Another lightning bolt, this time striking just south of the island, and another spume of water. The crowd begins to writhe and gibber, as though it were a single, plasmic organism.

>wait
Time passes.

Michael makes a three-quarter turn to his left, coming all the way around to face the lighthouse once more.

"To the Ultimate West, wherein lies the Void That Conquers All, I summon Thee!"

The top of the lighthouse begins to glow with a garish, violet light. An electric charge fills the air, prickling across your skin and filling your nose with a bitter ozone stink.

>wait
Time passes.

The air around the lighthouse is rippling now. Several cultists fall convulsing to the mud, while others scream in strange, strangled tongues. The earth shakes, and the air is split by a high-pitched harmonic ringing, like a crystal on the verge of shattering.

Michael raises the torch, preparing to thrust it into the pyre at the child's feet. He throws his head back and shrieks directly into the eye of the storm:

"IACH! IACH! ULUTUK FH'TAGHN!! BY THE KEYS OF IOK-SOTOT I NAME THEE!

"!!! IALDABAOLOTH !!! COME FORTH !!!"

Nothing happens.

Michael stares up at the brightly shining lighthouse, his face bathed in its purple radiance, his lunatic grin of triumph frozen in place, for the moment only vaguely cognizant that something has gone wrong.

"The crystal . . ." he whispers.

And then, just as the high-pitched ringing sound reaches an ear-splitting crescendo, the upper half of the lighthouse explodes.

Bits of burning debris rain down on the island. The cultists fall back in panic and start pushing at each other to get off the island and escape. Many fall into the water, where clusters of translucent, stringy tendrils immediately drag them screaming beneath the waves. A chunk of masonry the size of a pumpkin strikes one of your guards in the head, and he drops to the mud next to you with a mass of chewed-spaghetti pulp where his face used to be. The other guard takes a quick, frightened look around, then bolts without a backward glance.

>wait
Time passes.

Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning.

The fleshy ground shudders horribly beneath your feet. Bubbles begin to boil up around the island's edges.

"Come back!" screams Michael. "Come back, cowards!" No one heeds him. With a strangled curse he turns and hurls the torch into the sea. Then he turns to you.

"You . . . I should gut you here and now . . ." Suddenly the ground lurches, and Michael smiles. "But perhaps I won't need to." With a mocking bow, he strides past you to the gangplank leading back to shore. "I'm sorry I can't stay to watch you die," he calls over his shoulder, "but I have some rather urgent business to attend to." The next moment he is gone, running down the breakwater towards town.

>unlock handcuffs with needle
The steel cuffs bite painfully into your wrists, but with a little bit of excruciating contortion you manage to insert the end of the needle into the keyhole. For what seems like endless minutes you blindly jiggle the needle around, biting your lip against the cramps seizing your wrists and bitterly reflecting that you actually have no evidence, outside of watching action movies, that this stunt is even possible — when suddenly the cuffs snap open and fall from your wrists.

The boiling is becoming more violent. You can't help but notice that the tide seems to be striking the island a bit higher than before, even though the waves themselves don't seem to be getting any taller.

The boy squirms against the ties that still bind him to the wooden stake.

>untie boy
As quickly as you can, you loosen the cords binding the boy's wrists to the stake. As his hands slip free he jumps away from you and eyes you warily, sniffling and rubbing his arms.

Without a backward glance, he scurries off to the west.

The island is definitely sinking. Waves are lapping up almost to the island's center, and the near end of the gangplank is half-floating in the water.

>w
You nimbly step across the gangplank to the rocky spur.

Rocky Spur
A tiny spit of stone, barely large enough for you to stand on, juts out from the end of the breakwater. The hungry sea laps at your feet, surging over the rocks and trickling down into the crevices. To the southwest, an uneven trail leads back around the lighthouse.

To the east, just off the rocky spur, a small island is swiftly sinking into the ocean. A wooden gangplank lies across the gap.

Jeffrey Greer is here. Without waiting to see if you are following, he hurries off to the southwest.

A gust of wind blows your hair into your face.

With a final, shivering heave, the island of flesh sinks beneath the waves. Soon, nothing is left except the bubbling, turbulent patch of water.

>sw
The path turns west, curving around the side of the lighthouse.

Foot of the Lighthouse
You stand in a circular clearing among the stones, surrounded on nearly every side by the sea. Before you looms the lighthouse, a massive pillar of ancient, pale bricks, now broken and open to the sky. The road leads southwest from here, and a path leads around the outside of the lighthouse to the east.

The great, bronze door of the lighthouse stands slightly ajar.

Jeffrey Greer is here. Without a backward glance, he sprints off to the southwest.

The rain beats down ceaselessly.

>sw

The Spine
You are picking your way across the rough, rock-strewn spine of the breakwater, where the road has diminished to little more than a pair of muddy ruts. A hundred yards to the northeast, at the breakwater's far end, a ruined lighthouse stands vigil against the ocean.

Jeffrey Greer is here. Heedless of the wind and the rain, he scurries off to the west.

>w

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

Jeffrey Greer is here. Without waiting to see if you are following, he sprints off to the west.

>w

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

Jeffrey Greer is here. Without a backward glance, he runs off to the north.

>n

Shanty Town
The road from the south peters out at a small clearing of rutted mud, crowded close on all sides by leaning, dilapidated shacks.

The boy slowly approaches #38 Mill Town Road. For a moment, he stands there on the front stoop, spattered with mud, drenched by the rain. He turns to look back at you once. Then he raises his little fist and knocks on the door.

The door opens. The old woman looks out, then down. There is no pause, no moment of stunned recognition; she simply drops to her knees and folds him into her arms as if she had always been expecting him to come home at this exact moment. If she says anything, you cannot hear it over the rain, but she looks over the boy's shoulder and meets your eyes, and mouths the words, "Thank you."

Mother and son go inside, and the door closes behind them.

Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning.

>s

Mill Entrance
The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses.

>e

Mill Road
The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other.

>s

North Square
To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east.

At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university.

>s

Whateley Bridge
A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torrential water.

You can see your trenchcoat and a meat hook here.

>wear trenchcoat
(first taking the trenchcoat)
You put on the trenchcoat.

>s

Town Square
The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge.

In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. The old man's mutilated body hangs from its tip like a shattered marionette.

Michael stands next to the obelisk. He looks at you and smiles. It is not a particularly pleasant smile. He places his hand against the gore-streaked stone, then mutters something guttural under his breath. The bloody hieroglyphs begin to shine with an eerie phosphorescence which quickly brightens and envelopes Michael. His form wavers, then becomes transparent . . . and when the light fades, he has disappeared.

The obelisk continues to glow faintly.

>x obelisk
The obelisk measures about three feet square at its base, narrowing slightly as it rises a good fifteen feet to a blunt tip. It seems to be a monument of some sort, although you can see no plaque or marker anywhere near it. Dense, twisting hieroglyphs cover all four sides, partially obscured by rain-washed streaks of blood.

Embedded in the stone high up on one side, very near the top, is an iron ring, from which the old man's body hangs.

The obelisk is currently glowing with an eerie phosphorescence.

The rain slackens off momentarily to a weak drizzle, then returns afresh in a brief, freezing downpour.

The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat.

The obelisk continues to glow faintly.

>touch obelisk
As your fingers touch the blood-streaked stone, the light grows brighter, fanning out to envelope you. The odor of rotting fruit briefly assails your nostrils; your vision streaks, then blurs; there is the sound of rushing air, and when your eyes clear, you are somewhere else . . .

Burial Mound
A pale, watery glow sifts down from somewhere above, illuminating a cavern transformed into a warehouse of the dead. Hundreds of mummified human corpses lie on stone shelves carved into the walls. An altar of black stone, flanked on either side by a pair of metal pillars, sits at the north end of the gigantic cavern.

The base of the obelisk rises up before you, vast and shadowy in the shifting light.

Michael is here.

Suddenly Michael lunges forward and grabs your arms. He shakes you, causing your head to whip back and forth and your teeth to click painfully together.

"Bitch," he hisses, "you wretched, puling, conniving whore . . . you never give up, do you? You never stop! There's no way to fix what you've undone tonight, but by every god of the outer darkness, I will put you in the cold ground for it!"

Michael's hands lock tightly around your throat and begin to squeeze.

>show ring to michael
Desperate to reach whatever faint, guttering flicker of your husband's mind still survives beneath his madness . . . to just make him recognize you . . . you raise your left hand, holding your wedding ring up directly in front of Michael's face.

Michael is transfixed. He stares at the ring, its gold gleaming strangely in the shifting green light, with animalistic revulsion. For a moment you think he is going to let you go simply to get away from your hand. And then suddenly, his face spasms, rapidly shifting through extremes of confusion, fear, vulnerability, and anguish — all superimposed over something else, something darker and harder, trying to regain control.

Michael's grip loosens — not by much, but enough for you to drag a ragged whistle of air down your throat. For one blessed moment, you can breathe again.

>put amulet on michael
(first taking it off)
Seizing your chance, you slip the amulet's cord around Michael's neck, letting the silver hand-shaped pendant drop to his chest.

Michael reels backwards, howling and clawing at his chest as though the amulet were red-hot. Suddenly his body goes rigid. His back arches, his jaw stretches wide . . . and a thick, ropy stream of ectoplasmic mist erupts from his mouth. The stuff twists and clots in the air like wax poured into water, then coalesces into a human form — the vaporous, half-rotted apparition of Croseus Verlac.

The spectre laughs with a voice like pulling out rusty nails. "You sad, stupid woman," it creaks. "Only now do you realize that there was never any way on earth you could have stopped me. I have lived and thrived for over four hundred years. Not even death may claim me!"

>play flute
(first taking the strange metal flute from your pocket)
As you lift the twisted flute to your lips, Croseus suddenly ceases his raving. "You dare not —"

You shut your eyes, take a deep breath . . . and blow.

Once again, the strange harmony of the flute blends with the atonal ringing coming from each of the two columns. Once again, the air above the altar wavers, twists, then splits.

The howling wind scours the cavern, pulling everything towards the pulsating tear hovering over the altar. You dive towards Michael and clutch a fold of his pants leg in a death grip; with the other hand you manage to find the crack at the base of the monolith again and jam your fingers into it.

Verlac is snatched up like a cobweb in a hurricane. Frantically he claws at the air, but there is nothing for his insubstantial fingers to cling to. Shrieking and cursing, he is dragged inexorably back, closer and closer to the portal, until the suction draws his corpus out into a long, trailing ribbon, like the thread of an unraveling sweater — and in the next moment he is gone forever, swallowed by the all-consuming fissure in reality.

It seems to go on endlessly: your eyes squeezed shut against the chaos, your screams inaudible over the wind. But eventually it subsides. The portal closes, the wind stops. The flying debris settles to the floor. Except for your ragged breathing and the pounding of your heart, all is still; except for Michael, you are alone.

Silence reigns.

There passes some time that your memory cannot account for. A brief period of blackness and oblivion, you and your unconscious husband, together in the subterranean vault. Perhaps you were unconscious for a time as well. You cannot remember. At some point you find the strength to lift Michael to his feet. You put his arm around your shoulders, and the two of you somehow stagger up the titan stairs to the surface. The ground begins rumbling just as you are exiting the house, but by that time Michael has recovered some of his senses and can walk a bit faster.

You make it down to the bend in the road overlooking the town just in time to see the town square collapse and the vile obelisk sink into the earth, dragging several adjacent buildings down along with it. A gas main ruptures under the courthouse, sending a geyser of flame high into the air, and soon the entire town is burning.

Behind you, a muffled explosion and a sudden warmth against your back bespeak a similar end to the Verlac family mansion. For a moment, driven by instinct to rescue your belongings, you almost turn back — but you stop. You turn away. And while Michael rests, you stand at the overlook and watch the sparks rise like fireflies, mingling with the stars in a clear, cloudless sky.

z




>n
You can barely feel yourself walking, as if some outer force were moving you of its own accord.

Bathroom
Your bathroom; an ordinary place. A normal, safe, familiar place.

The pregnancy test that you took several minutes ago sits on the counter.

You hear a fly buzzing around right over the back of your neck.

A cool wave of uneasiness washes slowly over you; you're not sure why.

>x test
It's an ordinary pregnancy test, the kind you can get at any drugstore. It has a little window that turns pink if you're going to have a baby.

"Well?" Michael calls excitedly from downstairs. "Have you looked yet?"

>x window
You stare down at the little plastic indicator, and an inexplicable horror seizes you as you recall Edward Verlac's last words:

And besides, who could say whether the devil might not concoct some new means of entering the world, through a new body — through that of one of my daughters, perhaps? The ritual has traditionally demanded a grandson, but Croseus was never one to let such a trivial inconvenience stop him. He would find a way. They are still his blood.

He always returns to his blood.

The window is pink.

From downstairs, you can hear Michael excitedly talking to himself.

"Oh, I hope it's a girl," he says. "I've always wanted to have a little girl."



    *** The End ***



